8.1.0 (2026-08-22)

    Engine:
	- Fix: T* had the wrong sign in every output FCC writes. The scale
	       temperature is T* = -(w'T')/u* = -H/(rho*cp*u*), and Fluxes23 (FCC)
	       computed it without the leading minus, so it returned the negative
	       of the quantity its own header names: EddyPro +0.0966893 against
	       this engine -0.0966890 on the same period, identical to six digits
	       and opposite in sign. Both RP sites - Fluxes0_rp, from the
	       covariance, and Fluxes23_rp, from the final H - always carried the
	       minus, so RP's own full_output and FLUXNET file were correct
	       throughout; FCC read RP's correctly signed value out of the
	       essentials record and then overwrote it.
	       Two columns move and no others: T* in full_output and TSTAR in the
	       FLUXNET file, in the FCC-written copies only, each to exactly its
	       own negative. Nothing consumes the value - the one reader,
	       DevelopedTurbulenceTest, takes dabs() of it - so no flux, no
	       correction and no quality flag changes. This is our bug, not a
	       difference from EddyPro.
	       check_engines.py listed T* as a known ungated difference on the
	       reasoning that a sign convention is not a disagreement about the
	       physics. It was not a convention. The entry is gone and T* is now
	       gated with H and air_heat_capacity, the two quantities it is built
	       from; measured at 3e-6 against EddyPro, the last printed digit.
	- New feature: biom_use_native_header (SCTags(58), default on - unchanged
	               behaviour) lets external-CSV biomet get its per-channel
	               description from a sidecar .metadata file instead of the
	               file's own two-line header, the same key=value format
	               embedded .ghg biomet already reads via ReadBiometMetaFile.
	               Closes the gap BiometApplyCalibration's own note flagged:
	               external biomet had no way to state _gain/_offset because
	               InitExternalBiomet derived everything from the header
	               (RetrieveExtBiometVars); routing it through the metadata-
	               file path when the option is off gives it the same
	               calibration and channel description embedded biomet has
	               always had.
	               InitExternalBiomet now reads the sidecar file once, ahead
	               of the per-file size loop (so its record count and
	               bVars/bAggr* allocations are already settled going in),
	               and each subsequent file's own header lines are skipped
	               by count rather than parsed for variable names. At the
	               option's default (on), the header is read exactly as
	               before - this is a pure addition, not a rewrite of the
	               existing path.
	               Regression-swept: 28 fixtures, byte-identical.
	- New feature: Two sonic hardware corrections on the raw wind, both applied
               before any rotation and both off by default: tilt_sensor_meth,
               an inclinometer tilt correction, and head_corr_meth, the Metek
               USA-1 three-dimensional head correction.
               A planar fit or a double rotation removes the MEAN tilt over an
               averaging period. Neither can remove a tilt that changes WITHIN
               one. An inclinometer logged at the wind's own rate can, sample
               by sample, and that is what tilt_sensor_meth does - EddyUH's
               EC_Software_Common/EddyUH_tiltangle.m.
               The angles arrive as ordinary extra raw columns named theta, phi
               and psi, read through the same custom-column machinery that
               already carries the signal-strength channels. No new record
               family: there is one sonic, so a name is enough to say which
               instrument an angle belongs to. The columns hold the
               inclinometer's output VOLTAGE, not an angle; tilt_sensor_v_g
               says how many volts per g, defaulting to EddyUH's own 4.
               psi is read and then discarded, because EddyUH overwrites it
               with zeros and comments "not measured". So this is a two-angle
               correction with three channels declared.
               tilt_lpf_s smooths the angle series before it is used; zero, the
               default, does not. tilt_arm_x/y/z is the lever arm from the
               pivot to the head, read only by the swinging mode.
               head_corr_meth applies Metek's wind-tunnel tables for the flow
               distortion the transducers and their supports cause - three
               tables of Fourier coefficients over elevation, evaluated at
               three, six and nine times the azimuth (Functions_Library/
               METEK_HC.m). Two modes: on raw data, or on data that already
               carries Metek's online two-dimensional correction, which is
               undone first with the closed form Metek publishes for it.
               THE TABLES ARE NOT SHIPPED. phicorr.dat, ucorr.dat and
               alphacorr.dat are Metek GmbH's measurements, which EddyUH
               redistributes under the University of Helsinki's own agreement
               and this program cannot. head_corr_dir names a directory holding
               your own copy; without all three the correction declines for the
               whole run and says so, rather than correcting some periods and
               not others.
               The head correction runs in all three of RP's raw-data passes,
               as the angle-of-attack calibration beside it does. The
               inclinometer correction reaches the flux loop only: the two
               pre-passes run before DefineUserSet, so the angle columns do not
               exist yet. A plane is therefore fitted to mean winds this
               correction has not touched. Second order - it is aimed at
               variation within a period, not at the mean - but real, and left
               standing because closing it means moving DefineUserSet, a layout
               change to the one part of the program where a layout fault is
               silent.
               Neither correction affects CH-LAE, which runs a Gill HS with
               tiltcorr = 0. With the keys absent the output is byte-identical,
               confirmed on base_n_gas_analytic, base_5gas and base_cec_cos
               against binaries built from e847a12. Verified instead against
               hand-computed expectations: unit tables reconstruct the wind
               exactly, a speed table of 1.1 scales the vector by 1.1, a
               90-degree azimuth table sends (u,v) to (-v,u), the elevation
               grid interpolates halfway between two rows, and a 30-degree
               inclination sends (1,0,0) to (cos30, 0, -sin30).
	- Note: EddyUH's swinging correction adds a SCALAR to all three wind
       components, and EddyFlow reproduces that. EddyUH_tiltangle.m:104 writes
       omega*T*L with omega 1x3, T 3x3 and L = [-1.5 -1.5 -1.5]' 3x1, so the
       product is one number. The velocity of a point on a rotating body is
       omega CROSS (T L), a vector with three different components. The units
       survive - radians per second times metres is metres per second - which
       is why it is easy to miss.
       Reproduced as written, because the option exists to reproduce EddyUH's
       numbers and a silently corrected version would reproduce nothing.
       tilt_sensor_meth = 1, the position correction alone, is unaffected and
       is the one to use if EddyUH fidelity is not the goal. No lever arm turns
       a scalar into a vector.
	- Note: METEK_HC.m:81 and :90 index one row past the end of a twenty-row
       table at exactly +45 degrees of elevation - I = fix((phiM+50)/5)+1 gives
       20 there, and Cf0(I+1) is then Cf0(21). MATLAB raises rather than
       reading rubbish, so it is a crash and not a silent error, and an
       elevation of exactly 45.000 degrees is rare enough that it may never
       have been hit. EddyFlow holds the last row instead.
	- New feature: The spectral correction and the two flux levels can be repeated
	               until the stability they assume and the stability they produce
	               agree: corr_iter_meth, off by default, with corr_iter_max and
	               corr_iter_tol.
	               The three depend on each other in a circle. The analytic
	               cospectrum is evaluated at z/L; z/L comes from the corrected
	               sensible heat flux; that flux is what the spectral correction
	               produces. One pass leaves them disagreeing - the correction was
	               computed at a stability the run then went on to revise.
	               Nothing compounds. Fluxes1_rp clears Flux1 and rebuilds it from
	               Flux0 and BPCF; Fluxes23_rp clears Flux2 and Flux3 and rebuilds
	               them from Flux1. Each pass corrects the same raw covariances
	               afresh, so what changes between passes is only which z/L the
	               cospectrum was evaluated at. The reset at the head of each
	               routine is what makes that true, and a check asserts it - not the
	               absence of self-reference, which Fluxes23_rp legitimately has
	               within a single call.
	               The feedback path was already there: Fluxes23_rp recomputes
	               Ambient%zL, and the correction reads it. Only the repetition was
	               missing. FCC needed one more thing - its correction is handed
	               lEx%Flux0%zL while its Fluxes23 writes lEx%zL, two different
	               fields, so the loop threads the stability through a local rather
	               than letting it read the record every time.
	               corr_iter_dev, in percent, reports how far the gas fluxes moved
	               between the last two passes - the worst gas of the period. Worst
	               rather than one column per gas because every gas is corrected at
	               the same z/L, so what the loop converges is the stability and the
	               per-gas numbers move together. Written only when the loop runs,
	               like the random-error columns.
	               Verified on CH-LAE, 48 periods, on the analytic correction path.
	               With the loop off the output is byte-identical, confirmed on
	               base_n_gas_analytic, base_5gas and base_cec_cos. With it on, the
	               fluxes move by hundredths of a percent - latent heat +0.023 %,
	               nitrous oxide +0.038 %, carbonyl sulfide -0.022 %, sensible heat
	               essentially not at all - and the reported convergence has a
	               median of 0 %, a worst period of 0.35 %, and exactly one period
	               still moving more than 0.1 % at the fourth pass. A near-neutral
	               forest day is where this should do least; the difference is
	               largest in strongly non-neutral conditions, where the correction
	               factor is most sensitive to z/L.
	               A 1 % tolerance exits at the second pass and lands within 0.02 %
	               of the full four, which is the trade it exists to offer.
	- Doc: This repository said EddyUH iterates "until the flux changes by
	       <= 1 %", and attributed the loop to EddyUH_Spectralcorr.m. Both
	       halves were wrong, and the claim is retracted in
	       docs/eddyuh-comparison.md.
	       The loop is in EddyUH.m, which calls EddyUH_Spectralcorr.m. And
	       there is NO convergence test: `while indexITER <= 3` with the
	       counter incremented at the top runs exactly four times, always.
	       The only break (EddyUH.m:843) tests the urban footprint's
	       displacement height and roughness length, requires indexITER > 3
	       - true on the final pass only, where the loop ends anyway - and
	       sits in a branch EddyUH_footprint.m:151 makes unreachable.
	       The 1 % came from covsvar, which EddyUH computes as the
	       percentage change between the last two passes and writes to its
	       output. Nothing reads it back.
	       This matters for the defaults: corr_iter_tol = 0 means "run every
	       pass", which is what reproduces EddyUH. A tolerance is this
	       engine's own addition and departs from it.
	- Fix: The conditional lag borrowing can now use EddyUH's own rules:
	       tlag_borrow_noise chooses the noise floor a covariance is judged
	       against, and tlag_borrow_donor chooses who a gas that fails
	       borrows from. Both DEFAULT to this engine's own choice, so a
	       project that already had borrowing switched on keeps exactly what
	       it had - confirmed identical across every time-lag column on the
	       CH-LAE comparison run.
	       This corrects a fidelity error in the original port. EddyUH
	       compares |cov| against 3 x unc3(:,3), the Lenschow instrumental
	       noise, and borrows specifically from the analyser's carbon
	       dioxide, hard-coded by variable name at EddyUH_SC_Flux2.m:325-331.
	       The port compared against the Wienhold detection limit and
	       borrowed from the best-resolved tube-mate. EddyUH's own inline
	       comment at that line calls unc3 "the detection limit", which is
	       how the mistake was made; the two are both noise floors in
	       covariance units, but one measures the ANALYSER and the other
	       measures the COVARIANCE.
	       Only the detection-limit floor needs a second setting switched
	       on. It is computed elsewhere and read from Essentials, so asking
	       for it without detlim_meth would divide by a number nothing
	       produced. The Lenschow floor is measured here from the series in
	       hand, which means EddyUH's combination can be selected on its
	       own. The interface draws the same distinction and keeps the floor
	       control reachable even when the current choice is the unavailable
	       one, so a user whose detection limit is off can still reach the
	       control that fixes it.
	       The carbon-dioxide donor refuses rather than falling back. If
	       that analyser measures no carbon dioxide, nothing is borrowed:
	       the point of the rule is a shared tube, and another instrument's
	       gas shares only a clock. It also still requires the donor to have
	       cleared the threshold itself - a deliberate departure from
	       EddyUH, which copies the carbon dioxide lag unconditionally.
	       Copying a donor that could not resolve its own peak would launder
	       one bad detection onto every gas on the tube.
	       LenschowFluxNoise is now a function of its own, called both by
	       the random-uncertainty arm and from here. One implementation,
	       because the two must divide by the same number and a drift
	       between them would surface as a lag population rather than as an
	       uncertainty column. The extraction is numerically inert: every
	       rand_err_ column is identical to the previous engine's.
	       Verified on CH-LAE, 48 periods, with the baseline-subtracted
	       maximisation on. Under EddyUH's rules carbon dioxide stops
	       borrowing entirely - 6 of 48 periods down to 0, since it is now
	       the donor and cannot borrow from itself - nitrous oxide falls
	       from 8 to 2 and carbonyl sulfide from 18 to 15. Fewer borrows
	       because the Lenschow floor is the smaller of the two, so more
	       gases clear three times it: instrument noise alone is less than
	       the scatter of a whole cross-covariance function, which also
	       carries the atmosphere.
	- New feature: Instrumental noise after Lenschow et al. (2000), as Mauder et al.
	               (2013) apply it: ru_meth = 5, one more choice on the existing
	               random-uncertainty method. White instrument noise is
	               uncorrelated between samples, so it lands entirely in the
	               autocovariance at zero lag and nowhere else. Fit a line through
	               lags 1 to 5, read it back at zero, and the gap between that line
	               and the measured lag-zero value is the noise variance; the flux
	               noise is sqrt(g_noise * var_w / N).
	               It answers a DIFFERENT QUESTION from the three beside it.
	               Finkelstein & Sims and Mann & Lenschow give a sampling error -
	               how much this half hour's covariance would differ from another
	               draw. Billesbach gives a floor below which a flux cannot be told
	               from zero. This gives the ANALYSER's own noise, and says nothing
	               about whether the atmosphere was sampled long enough. They are
	               not interchangeable and the tooltip says so.
	               Not the same paper as ru_meth = 2 either. mann_lenschow_94 is a
	               sampling error from Mann & Lenschow (1994); the shared surname is
	               all the two have in common, which is exactly why the method here
	               is named lenschow_00 rather than anything shorter.
	               It DECLINES a period rather than guessing. If either intercept
	               comes out non-positive the fitted line already sits at or above
	               the measured lag-zero value, which is not a small noise estimate -
	               it is the method failing to apply, and reporting it as a small
	               number would be worse than reporting nothing. The vertical wind
	               gates every gas, since a w autocovariance with no noise-like step
	               breaks the assumption whatever the scalar did.
	               The five-lag window is in SAMPLES, as EddyUH has it - fixed by
	               the method rather than derived from the acquisition rate, so it
	               spans 0.5 s at 10 Hz and half that at 20. Reproduced as it
	               stands: the number of lags is what the extrapolation is
	               conditioned on, and deriving it from the rate would be a
	               different method wearing the same citation.
	               Verified on CH-LAE, 48 periods, against the other two estimators.
	               It is the smallest for the strong fluxes - sensible heat 0.21
	               against Billesbach's 1.72 and Finkelstein & Sims's 23.1 - and
	               converges on Billesbach for the weak ones: nitrous oxide 0.0685
	               against 0.0663, carbonyl sulfide 0.0206 against 0.0184. That
	               convergence is the check that matters. Billesbach's shuffle
	               destroys every correlation, so for a series whose variance IS
	               instrument noise it measures the same quantity this does by a
	               completely different route; the two agreeing to a few percent
	               where noise dominates, and diverging by a factor of two where
	               atmospheric variance dominates, is what the physics requires.
	               It declined 15 of 48 periods for the MIRO channels and 36 to 40
	               for the LI-7200's, which is the low-noise instrument doing what
	               the gate exists to catch: a small lag-zero step is easy for a
	               five-point extrapolation to overshoot. That is the honest answer
	               for such an analyser, not a defect.
	               With the method not selected the output is byte-identical -
	               confirmed on base_n_gas_ru and base_5gas.
	- New feature: Consecutive-difference despiking, EddyUH's spi_method 1:
	               despike_vm = 2, beside Vickers & Mahrt and Mauder. If a sample
	               steps further from the one before it than a stated limit, it is
	               replaced by its predecessor and counted as a spike. A
	               rate-of-change limit, NOT a statistical outlier test - nothing
	               is scaled by a standard deviation and nothing iterates, which
	               is the whole difference between it and the two beside it.
	               This is the method the CH-LAE carbonyl sulfide project ran, and
	               it was missed the first time round: the earlier pass scoped
	               itself to capabilities EddyFlow lacked entirely and filed this
	               under "same intent, different implementation", where it sat
	               without being acted on. It is not the same intent. Until now a
	               run configured to match that project was despiked by a
	               different rule than the one that produced the fluxes it was
	               being compared against.
	               The limits are ABSOLUTE, in each variable's own unit: four
	               sonic keys - sr_step_u, sr_step_v, sr_step_w, sr_step_ts -
	               rather than the two the Vickers sigma multipliers share, and
	               gas_<i>_step_lim per gas. Sharing one key across u and the
	               sonic temperature would put a kelvin and a metre per second
	               behind the same number.
	               Zero means the column is not despiked, which is the shape
	               EddyUH's dlim has - a NaN entry there means the same - and the
	               run log names every column it passed over for that reason. A
	               method that silently does nothing when nobody filled in its
	               numbers is worse than one that says so.
	               Where it differs from EddyUH deliberately: EddyUH replaces a
	               NaN by its predecessor and counts that as a spike, before any
	               method runs. This does not. A gap is a gap, the engine has its
	               own machinery for it, and folding gaps into the spike count
	               would make the spike percentage - which drives the hard flag -
	               a measure of two things at once. Comparison resumes from the
	               next valid pair rather than manufacturing a step across a gap.
	               The historical values of despike_vm decode exactly as before:
	               '0' was Vickers and ANYTHING else was Mauder, so the default
	               arm stays Mauder rather than becoming the nominal default. Only
	               '2' behaves differently from before, which is what makes an
	               existing project byte-identical - confirmed on base_5gas,
	               base_cec_cos and base_n_gas_analytic.
	               Verified on CH-LAE, 48 periods, with EddyUH's own dlim values
	               for that project. It removes 23 fewer u spikes, 71 fewer w, 20
	               fewer sonic temperature and 18 fewer carbon dioxide than Mauder
	               does, and 624 more on the MIRO's water channel.
	               Worth knowing, and not obvious until it was run: at those
	               values the method finds NOTHING in nitrous oxide (80 ppb limit
	               on a 330 ppb signal), carbonyl sulfide (0.7 ppb on 0.44 ppb),
	               or either LI-7200 channel. Tightening the nitrous oxide limit
	               to 0.5 ppb and carbonyl sulfide to 0.02 ppb still finds
	               nothing; only at 1e-4 do they respond, which is the check that
	               the per-gas limits reach the routine at all. So EddyUH's own
	               configuration despiked the sonic and two of six gas channels
	               and left the rest untouched - including the two species the
	               project exists for.
	- Doc: A stability-binned planar fit was NOT added, and the reason is
	       worth keeping. EddyUH can compute one - planar_fit_coeff.m case
	       0 - but cannot apply one: EddyUH_coordrot.m selects the bin from
	       its sixth argument, and all four call sites in the package pass
	       NaN, including the flux calculation itself. With NaN the search
	       finds no bin and neither out-of-range branch fires, so the plane
	       coefficients are never assigned and the next line reads them.
	       Selecting stability bins there does not change a flux; it stops
	       with an undefined variable. nobin and WDbin assign
	       unconditionally and are unaffected.
	       Nor was it used at this site. The planar-fit file holds a single
	       nobin plane, w = 0.0845 - 0.3624u + 0.0559v, and the setup then
	       runs Dim_coordrot = 2 - double rotation - so that plane never
	       reached a flux either.
	       And nothing is missing here in any case: EddyUH's nobin is one
	       plane fitted to everything, which is what this engine computes
	       for a single wind sector of 360 degrees. read_ini_rp.f90 forces
	       wsect_end(num_sec) = 360 and WindSector puts every direction in
	       sector 1 when num_sec is 1.
	       The obstacle, if it is ever wanted anyway, is architectural.
	       The rotation runs at eddyflow-rp_main.f90:2171 and Ambient%zL is
	       first computed in Fluxes0_rp at :2536, so the stability that
	       would choose the bin does not exist when the bin must be chosen;
	       the planar-fit assessment pass keeps only the mean wind per
	       period and never forms a heat flux at all. Both would need a
	       provisional double-rotated stability computed for the purpose,
	       which is a design decision with no working precedent to copy.
	       Written up in docs/eddyuh-comparison.md. Third finding of this
	       shape, after the analyser crosstalk and the spectral-assessment
	       cospectral model: the file is there, the mathematics is written
	       down, and nothing reaches it.
	- New feature: The analytic cospectrum every spectral correction is integrated
	               against can now be chosen: cosp_model, defaulting to the
	               Moncrieff et al. (1997) curve this program has always used.
	               Kaimal et al. (1972), Sakai et al. (2001), Su et al. (2003),
	               Moraes et al. (2008) and Kristensen et al. (1997) are the
	               alternatives.
	               It is a MODIFIER on whichever correction method is selected, not
	               a method of its own. Every analytic path - Moncrieff, Massman,
	               Horst, Ibrom, Fratini and the low-pass-only case - weights a
	               transfer function by this shape, so it decides how much of the
	               flux is assumed to sit where the instrument attenuates, and so
	               how large the correction comes out. On the analytic regression
	               fixture the six models spread the gas correction factors from
	               1.0170 to 1.0557 against Moncrieff's 1.0335 - about four
	               percent of the correction, a few tenths of a percent of the
	               flux.
	               Only the SHAPE matters: the factor is IntCO / IntTFCO, a ratio
	               of two integrals of the same cospectrum, so any constant
	               multiplying it divides out. That is why Kristensen's 2*pi/z
	               prefactor is dropped, and why the numerically integrated
	               normalisation EddyUH applies to it - which depends on hard-coded
	               indices into whatever frequency vector it is handed - is not
	               reproduced. Neither could change a correction factor.
	               The Reynolds stress keeps Moncrieff's momentum cospectrum
	               whichever option is chosen. Sakai, Su, Moraes and Kristensen are
	               scalar cospectra with no momentum form, and pairing a chosen
	               scalar model with an unrelated momentum one for some options and
	               not others would be worse than not offering the choice. Tau_scf
	               is identical across all six on the fixture, which is the check
	               that this holds.
	               Four of the six have no stability branch at all - a single form
	               EddyUH applies at any zL, and this does the same. Under stable
	               stratification the cospectral peak moves to higher frequency, so
	               a neutral-form model puts too little flux there and will
	               UNDERSTATE the high-frequency loss. Said in the interface
	               tooltip as well.
	               Worth knowing: EddyUH's CMoore, after Moore (1986), is
	               term-for-term the curve this program calls Moncrieff et al.
	               (1997) - scalar and momentum both. The two names are one
	               cospectrum. Kaimal et al. (1972) is a genuinely different curve,
	               and different again from the column the spectral assessment
	               output labels kaimal_cosp, which kaimal() in kaimal_models.f90
	               computes and which is also Moore's.
	               The formulae are EddyUH's modelcospectra.m, checked against it
	               literal by literal in the static check. EddyUH uses them for
	               diagnostic plots, not for its own correction - it corrects
	               against measured and fitted cospectra - so this is EddyUH's
	               model library and not EddyUH's method.
	               With the key absent the output is byte-identical to the previous
	               engine on base_n_gas_analytic and base_5gas: the dispatcher
	               calls the Moncrieff routine unconditionally and its select has
	               no branch for moncrieff_97, so the default cannot be reached
	               through the selector at all.
	- New feature: A gas whose cross-covariance cannot be told from noise may take
	               its time lag from another gas on the same analyser instead of
	               inventing one of its own: tlag_borrow_meth, off by default. Species
	               drawn down one tube share a transport delay, so the strongest peak
	               in the bundle is the best estimate of it. Nemitz et al. (2018) make
	               this the rule for trace gases measured alongside a well-resolved
	               one, which is exactly the case here - carbonyl sulfide, nitrous
	               oxide and carbon dioxide on a single MIRO.
	               The test is the flux detection limit, so this needs detlim_meth =
	               wienhold_94 and the engine refuses the combination without it. A gas
	               whose |covariance| stands at least tlag_borrow_snr detection limits
	               above the noise - 3 by default, the paper's value - keeps its own
	               lag and is eligible to donate. One that does not, or whose maximum
	               landed on an end of the search window where a maximisation goes when
	               there is no interior peak to find, borrows from the eligible gas on
	               the same analyser with the highest ratio. The trusted set is
	               snapshotted before any borrowing, so a borrowed lag can never itself
	               become a donor, and the outcome does not depend on column order.
	               Water is never borrowed for or from: its lag is the one every other
	               gas's water covariance is taken at, and moving it would move the
	               water term of every WPL correction with it.
	               A borrowed lag is flagged in <gas>_def_timelag exactly as the
	               nominal fallback is - it is not a detected lag - and the donor is
	               named in the run log.
	               On the test day, with the baseline-subtracted maximisation on, 31 of
	               the 144 gas-periods borrowed and the within-period spread of the
	               three MIRO lags fell from 0.75 s to 0.40 s. Carbonyl sulfide clears
	               three detection limits in only 10 of 48 periods there, which is the
	               honest measure of how little of its lag was ever its own.
	- New feature: The time lag may be chosen by the largest departure of the
	               cross-covariance from the straight line joining the two ends of the
	               search window, rather than by its largest absolute value:
	               covmax_debaseline, off by default. A weak flux often sits on a sloping
	               cross-covariance - from a trend, or from a neighbouring stronger
	               correlation - and the plain maximum then lands on whichever end the
	               slope is highest at instead of on the peak; subtracting the chord takes
	               the slope away and leaves the peak.
	               A MODIFIER on covariance maximisation, not a method of its own. A sixth
	               tlag_meth would force a decision in the EddyPro importer, which must
	               keep mapping EddyPro's tlag_meth=2 onto plain maxcov, and EddyPro has no
	               such criterion. It changes which lag is selected and nothing about the
	               covariance reported there.
	               This is what EddyUH actually runs at this site: EddyUH_SC_Flux.m:281
	               computes it under the menu entry labelled "standard", with the plain
	               maximum commented out immediately above. Switching it on moves the lag
	               populations towards EddyUH's own climatology - carbonyl sulfide from
	               15.927 to 16.048 s against EddyUH's 16.017, carbon dioxide on the MIRO
	               from 15.975 to 16.023 against 16.015, and the LI-7200's carbon dioxide
	               from 0.629 to 0.648 against 0.651 - with comparable scatter. Nitrous
	               oxide moves slightly the other way. One day against a climatology
	               computed over a different season, so this is an order-of-magnitude
	               agreement and not a validation.
	               Worth knowing: with the baseline removed the two ends of the window
	               score zero by construction, so the maximum can never land on one - and
	               maxcov&default falls back to the nominal lag precisely when it does. On
	               the test day the fallback fired for 9 to 38 periods per gas with the
	               modifier off and for none with it on. For a weak flux that safety net
	               is worth replacing rather than simply losing, which is what the
	               detection-limit-conditional borrowing is for.
	- Fix: CovMax no longer returns an undefined row lag. RLag is intent(out) and was
	               assigned only inside the comparison, so a window in which nothing beat
	               the initial zero - every covariance error-coded, or all of them exactly
	               zero - returned whatever was on the stack, and the caller then shifted
	               the series by it. Initialised to the start of the window.
	- Fix: CovMax no longer lets an error-coded covariance win the maximisation.
	               CovarianceMatrixNoError returns the error code when a lag leaves no
	               overlapping valid samples, and the comparison was on the magnitude:
	               abs(-9999) beats every real covariance in a window, so such a lag was
	               selected outright. Now skipped. Latent on well-formed data - the
	               regression is byte-identical - but a gas with a gap over part of its
	               search window would have had its lag pinned to wherever the gap was.
	- Doc: The spectroscopic coefficients are stated to be SPECTROSCOPIC ONLY, and the
	               conversion from EddyUH's is written down. Both packages divide by the
	               same polynomial, 1 + a*chi_q + b*chi_q^2, with the water in mol/mol -
	               the formulae are identical - but EddyUH folds the dilution into it:
	               Functions_Library/dilucorr.m uses a = -1, b = 0 for the pure-dilution
	               case, so every coefficient in its published table sits on a baseline of
	               a = -1. EddyFlow corrects the density separately, and more fully, so
	               its identity is a = b = 0.
	               A published value entered unconverted therefore counts the dilution
	               twice, and nothing about the two formulae makes that visible. The
	               mapping is a_here = a_EddyUH + 1, b_here = b_EddyUH + a_EddyUH + 1, and
	               the pure-dilution pair maps to (0, 0) exactly, as it must. EddyUH gives
	               -1.39 for nitrous oxide on an Aerodyne cw-QCL, which is -0.39 here.
	               Now in the routine's docstring, in both interface tooltips, and in
	               docs/eddyuh-comparison.md with the whole published table converted.
	               Pinned by static_checks/test_spectroscopic_static.py, because it is the
	               kind of note that gets tidied away.
	- Doc: EddyUH has no analyser-crosstalk correction, and the comparison document no
	               longer claims one. EddyUH_ctc_CP.m looks like one and is unreachable -
	               nothing calls it, and it would fail if anything did, multiplying a
	               scalar water value by a two-element coefficient vector.
	               crosstalk_coeff_CP.m is a table of SPECTROSCOPIC (a, b) pairs whose only
	               live callers are the two setup dialogs, seeding set_Gan.crosstalkcoeff -
	               which EddyUH_spect_CP.m and dilucorr.m then read as their a and b. The
	               crosstalkflag gates the spectroscopic correction. The name is
	               historical, and the capability is the one already implemented.
	- New feature: The spectroscopic effect of water vapour is removed from closed-path
	               laser analysers, off unless asked for. Water broadens the absorption
	               lines a laser measures, so what it reports scales as 1 + a*chi_q +
	               b*chi_q^2 (Peltola et al. 2014) and the correction is a division by
	               that polynomial. Applied POINT BY POINT after Chen et al. (2010), using
	               the water the same analyser read at the same sample - the broadening
	               happens in the cell at that instant, so no time-lag alignment enters.
	               Two settings in RawProcess_Settings, spectro_meth (0 none, 1 chen_10)
	               and spectro_water, plus per-column coefficients col_<N>_spectro_a and
	               col_<N>_spectro_b in the metadata. All default to zero or off, and zero
	               coefficients make the polynomial one, so a project and a metadata file
	               written before any of this keep their numbers exactly.
	               On the series and not on the flux, for three reasons. The mean and the
	               covariance are corrected together and consistently. The full
	               nonlinearity is kept rather than a first-order term. And it reaches
	               gases reported as a mixing ratio - which Level 2 returns early on, so a
	               flux-level correction placed with the density terms would do NOTHING
	               for a laser reporting mixing ratios, the common case for carbonyl
	               sulfide and nitrous oxide and exactly the case this is for.
	               EddyUH's flux-level form folds the dilution in with the spectroscopy,
	               because its own WPL runs first and the formula partly undoes it;
	               substituting one into the other, the dilution cancels exactly and what
	               is left is the division above. EddyFlow's WPL is a fuller formulation -
	               it carries cell temperature and pressure terms EddyUH's closed-path WPL
	               does not - so only the spectroscopic part is taken and the density work
	               is left where it already is.
	               With a = -0.30 at this site's humidity the reported mixing ratio moves
	               0.45 %, which is what 1/(1 - 0.30*0.015) is; the flux moves 0.7 % for
	               carbonyl sulfide and 1.8 % for carbon dioxide, the difference being the
	               cross term, which is proportionally larger the smaller the flux.
	- New feature: The water channel can be corrected against its own reading, as
	               spectro_water, off by default. This is self-broadening and is NOT part
	               of the published Peltola et al. result, which derives the effect of
	               water on another gas's lines. EddyUH corrects it too, with a flux-level
	               coefficient whose derivation its own source comment says is not
	               published anywhere; offered here in the same point-by-point form as
	               every other column, behind its own switch, and labelled as such in the
	               interface so that nobody acquires it by accident.
	- New feature: Billesbach (2011) random-shuffle noise floor, selectable as
	               ru_meth = 4. Reordering a scalar at random destroys every real
	               correlation with the vertical wind, so whatever covariance survives the
	               shuffle was produced by noise alone; the mean of twenty such
	               covariances - EddyUH's count, and the paper's - is a floor below which
	               a flux cannot be told from zero. Reported through the existing
	               rand_err_* columns, so nothing about the output layout changes.
	               This is a NOISE FLOOR and not a sampling error. It answers whether a
	               flux is resolvable, not how uncertain it is, and is systematically the
	               smaller of the two: on the test dataset it runs at 6 to 13 per cent of
	               Finkelstein and Sims for the strong fluxes and 33 to 54 per cent for
	               nitrous oxide and carbonyl sulfide, which is the point - for a weak
	               flux the instrument is most of the uncertainty.
	               Four rather than three because three is already Mahrt (1998), and the
	               interface maps its menu onto these numbers; renumbering would have
	               silently changed the method of every project that states one.
	- Fix: The Billesbach routine has been unreachable and unusable since it was
	               written. It carried \todo Under development, had no arm in the dispatch,
	               and had five faults, each of which produces numbers rather than an
	               error:
	               (1) Its indexing was transposed. Set is declared Set(N, M) - records
	               down, variables across - and every other routine in the file indexes it
	               Set(:, var); this one used Set(w, 1:N), reading row w across N columns
	               of an array that has about seventy.
	               (2) It stored its result nowhere. The shuffled covariance went into
	               Stats%Cov and nothing read it back.
	               (3) That same line overwrote live state. Stats%Cov is filled by
	               BasicStats and read fifteen times by Fluxes0_rp a few hundred lines
	               later, so every flux in the period would have been computed from
	               shuffled data.
	               (4) Its shuffle was biased. RandomBetween computed
	               int((max - min) * x + min), which never returns max; its only caller is
	               a Fisher-Yates loop where j must be able to equal i, and unable to, the
	               element at i always moves - drawing from the permutations that leave
	               nothing in place rather than from all of them.
	               (5) Its generator was never seeded. InitRandomSeed is also dead code,
	               so the sequence came from whatever gfortran seeds itself with, which
	               since GCC 7 is the operating system: the same project over the same
	               data would have published a different noise floor every run. It is now
	               seeded once per run from a fixed value, which makes a run reproducible
	               while still leaving successive periods independent draws.
	               The routine also shuffles the scalar rather than the wind. Shuffling w
	               once and reusing it for every gas is cheaper, and is what the old
	               version did, but it makes every gas's estimate a draw from the same
	               realisation - they would rise and fall together for no physical reason.
	- New feature: A flux detection limit after Wienhold et al. (1994), off unless asked
	               for. The cross-covariance of w with a scalar carries the flux in a peak
	               near the transport lag and nothing but noise far from it, so the scatter
	               of the function out there is a noise floor on the covariance: a flux
	               smaller than it cannot be told from zero. Two windows are placed
	               symmetrically either side of the lag the run actually used for that gas
	               and their standard deviations averaged, which is what the paper does.
	               Reported per gas, in COVARIANCE units, as <gas>_detlim in the full output
	               and <TAG>_DETLIM in the FLUXNET file. Deliberately not scaled to a flux:
	               what it qualifies is the covariance, and carrying it through the spectral
	               correction as if it were a flux would invite a comparison that does not
	               hold.
	               Three settings, all in RawProcess_Settings because FCC never recomputes
	               the limit - it rides the ex record: detlim_meth (0 none, 1 wienhold_94,
	               default 0), detlim_offset_s (100) and detlim_window_s (50). The two
	               window settings carry Wienhold's own values so that switching the feature
	               on reproduces the published method rather than something chosen here; an
	               offset that does not clear the half-width would measure the peak instead
	               of the noise under it, and is refused back to the defaults.
	               Computed inside TimeLagHandle, beside the water covariances, and for the
	               same reason: it is read off the cross-covariance function, which exists
	               only while the series are still on their raw alignment. The routine that
	               follows shifts every column by its own lag, after which the function this
	               measures is gone. A call from the main program would have returned
	               plausible numbers that meant nothing.
	               On the test dataset this is the whole point: COS clears three times its
	               detection limit in 10 of 48 half-hours and N2O in 16, which is the
	               ordinary state of affairs for those species at a forest and was not
	               previously visible from an EddyFlow run at all.
	- Fix: CovarianceW accepts a negative lag. It ran `do i = 1, nrow - lag` and indexed
	               col2(i+lag) unguarded, which reads off both ends of the array when the
	               lag is negative; both callers of the day guarded with `if (lag <= 0)
	               cycle` and so never met it. The detection limit does - its earlier noise
	               window sits at a negative lag whenever the offset exceeds the transport
	               delay, which for a gas at 16 s sampled 100 s before it is -84 s. The
	               bounds now clamp both ends. A positive lag walks exactly the terms it
	               always did, so nothing an existing caller asks for has moved.
	- New feature: -j/--jobs splits the planar-fit and time-lag assessment pre-passes
	               across worker processes. Both walk every flux averaging period in
	               their range and append one record that depends on no other period's,
	               so the range is cut into contiguous slices, each slice runs in a copy
	               of eddyflow_rp, and the parent concatenates the slices in order and
	               hands the result to the unchanged fit. Processes rather than threads:
	               the period loop reaches most of the program's global state, and a
	               process has its own copy of all of it by construction.
	               -j 1 is the serial path this program has always taken; -j 0, and the
	               absent switch, use every core. The parent takes the first slice
	               itself, so it arrives at the fit with the state the loop establishes -
	               SortWindBySector reads the north offset out of E2Col, which is filled
	               when a raw file's metadata is read, and a parent that had skipped the
	               loop binned every period into a sector rotated by the site's true
	               offset.
	               Planar fit and the aggregate time-lag optimiser come out byte-identical
	               to the serial run: the records are fixed-size derived types with no
	               allocatable components, written unformatted and read back by the same
	               binary. On two days of CH-LAE the planar fit went 120 s to 22 s and the
	               time-lag optimiser 200 s to 57 s, both on eight workers.
	               The PWB cache pre-pass (tlag_meth=5, to_mode=1) is deliberately NOT
	               split, and runs exactly as it does today. Its classifier decides a
	               period partly from the last settled detection before it, and that
	               chain has no time limit: the streaming classifier sets its flag once
	               and never clears it.
	               A lead-in was built and measured before this was settled. It works in
	               principle - one S1_optimal detection is decided on the period's own
	               bootstrap interval alone and overwrites the carried lag, origin and
	               flag outright, so from the first S1 a cold worker and a serial run
	               are in the same state. It does not work in practice, because the weak
	               species never settle: over two days of CH-LAE, COS reached no settled
	               detection in 103 periods and nitrous oxide reached two, so no lead-in
	               of any length rebuilds their state.
	               Splitting it anyway left every applied lag identical period for
	               period, but moved donor attribution on 8 of 726 rows at one seam, and
	               the aggregate summary resolved from those donor votes moved with it -
	               two counts by one or two, one median lag by 0.05 s. Not worth having.
	- Fix: A switch written after the project path on the command line was silently
	               ignored. The argument loop read a value after every token, so the path
	               swallowed whatever followed it, and the lowercasing of the path ran on
	               the loop's own counter and left it past the end of the command line -
	               which ended the loop there and hid the first problem behind the second.
	               `eddyflow_rp <project> -e <home>` therefore ran with the default home
	               directory, not the one asked for. A switch now consumes a value only
	               when it takes one, and the loop reads to the end of the command line.
	- Fix: A spectral assessment output file that could not be opened was written
	               anyway, to a file nobody would look for. All five outputs in
	               OutputSpectralAssessmentResults did
	                   open(udf, file = FilePath, iostat = open_status)
	                   if (open_status /= 0) call ExceptionHandler(64)
	               and then wrote regardless: ExceptionHandler(64) only logs, and a
	               write to an unconnected unit does not fail - gfortran connects it
	               to fort.<udf> in whatever directory the run started in. udf is the
	               MILLISECOND the run began in, so the name carries no meaning either.
	               The message it printed, "Some spectral assessment results will not
	               be written on output file", was false: they were written, in full,
	               somewhere else. A 36 KB fort.615 of ensemble cospectra and Massman
	               fits had been sitting in the repository root since 2026-08-21.
	               The five sites now share OpenSpectralOutputFile, which sinks a
	               failed open to a scratch unit the caller's close discards. Returning
	               instead would be wrong - these are five separate files and one
	               failing to open says nothing about the next.
	               This is the third time this class has bitten: unit 11 in
	               edit_ini_file (fixed 2026-08-17), unit 163 in the run log, now udf.
	               test_stray_unit_writes_static.py now asserts that a failed open
	               guarded by a NON-FATAL handler cannot fall through into writes - the
	               fatal set is parsed out of exception_handler.f90 rather than listed,
	               so a handler that stops today and warns tomorrow comes into scope on
	               its own. fort.* is no longer in .gitignore: ignoring it is what let
	               this go unnoticed for weeks.
	- Change: .mod files are written straight to the object directory (-J), and
	               -fbounds-check has moved to a new `make debug` target.
	               The modules used to be written beside the makefile and swept across
	               by the LINK rule, so any build that stopped in between - a compile
	               error, a link error, Ctrl-C - left them there. clean_all did not
	               remove them and gfortran searches the current directory ahead of -I,
	               so a stale .mod could survive a clean and become the interface every
	               later compile bound against. A stray m_cec.mod in the repository
	               root had already made one compile fail with a type mismatch that
	               read like a source bug.
	               -fbounds-check cannot change a value, only turn a wrong subscript
	               into a stop, so a released binary does not need it. Removing it is
	               worth 6-7 % on the compute-bound passes - the time-lag optimiser
	               went 197 s to 183 s and the planar fit 108 s to 101 s over two days
	               of CH-LAE - with byte-identical output, which the flag guarantees
	               and both were checked to confirm. The whole sweep was run with it
	               still on first (25 passed) so that nothing was relying on it.
	- New feature: The compressed LI-COR GHG format has a regression fixture, and
	               the next archive is decompressed while the current one is being
	               processed.
	               Nothing exercised file_type=0 before this. It is the only raw path
	               that shells out to 7-Zip, and the only one that rewrites
	               Metadata%ac_freq, NumCol and FileInterpreter per file, because each
	               archive carries its own metadata - so the one place those globals
	               move under the period loop had no gate at all. base_ghg is the same
	               three hours as base_tlag_opt, built by gen_ghg.py, and the standard
	               it is held to is that its fluxes MATCH that fixture's, because it is
	               the same data. They do, to the byte.
	               Measured against it, a GHG file costs about 170 ms more than the
	               same data as CSV, of which roughly 100 ms is 7-Zip. That part is
	               time spent waiting on another process, so it is now spent computing
	               a flux instead: the archive the next period will want is extracted
	               in the background, and claimed only if the path matches exactly and
	               the extraction has signalled that it finished. Every other case -
	               no request, the wrong file, one still running, one that failed, a
	               directory a killed run left behind - falls through to extracting
	               synchronously exactly as before, so the worst a prefetch can do is
	               waste itself. -j 1 turns it off with everything else.
	               UnZipArchive also lists the extracted files once rather than twice.
	               Both listings only ever asked what the archive held, and each cost
	               a shell of its own.
	               Together these are worth about 2.8 % of a GHG run - 25.8 s to 25.1 s
	               over the fixture. Less than the 5-6 % first estimated: only the
	               extraction can be hidden, not the shell calls around it, which still
	               run in the main process. Output is byte-identical either way, which
	               was checked rather than assumed.
	- New feature: test_rf, off by default (RawProcess_Tests, RFlux-master
	               added to the repo as a reference). Adapted from RFlux's
	               inst_prob_test() (Vitale et al. 2020, Biogeosciences): lag-1
	               autocorrelation (AL1), the discrete/dominant-value test (DDI),
	               and spike counts on the raw series and its first differences
	               (HF5/HF10/HD5/HD10), scaled by a robust median/MAD estimator
	               rather than RFlux's Qn. qn_estimator.f90 already carries a Qn
	               implementation, but it is O(n^2) in both memory and time over
	               all sample pairs - at 20 Hz over 30 minutes that is ~6.5e8
	               pairs, well past what a per-period raw-level test can spend -
	               and it is not called from anywhere in the tree. A true
	               O(n log n) Qn is a separate follow-up; median/MAD plays the
	               same role at the cost this test can actually afford, the same
	               idiom TestSpikeDetectionMauder13 already uses.
	               KID (kurtosis of increments) already existed unconditionally,
	               unrelated to this flag - see kid.f90, added in 110bf29.
	               The new columns are appended at the very end of the FLUXNET
	               row, after the biomet block, deliberately: that tail is what
	               read_ex_record.f90 captures whole as fluxnetChunks%s(6) and
	               FCC re-emits verbatim. The LGD/KID/ZCD family a few columns
	               earlier is instead sized by a fixed field count
	               (nLgdFields) that this flag does not touch, so splicing new
	               columns in there would have silently misaligned every field
	               FCC parses positionally after them whenever the test is on.
	               Regression-swept with the flag at its default (off): 28
	               fixtures, byte-identical.
	- New feature: test_rf also computes DIP, Hartigan's dip test p-value
	               (Hartigan & Hartigan 1985; Hartigan 1985, AS 217) for
	               multimodality in the raw signal - RFlux's dip.test() call,
	               ported rather than re-derived: dip_test.f90 is a direct
	               translation of the greatest-convex-minorant/least-concave-
	               majorant algorithm from RUrlus/diptest's diptst() (itself a
	               C++ rewrite of R diptest's C code, an f2c translation of the
	               original AS 217 Fortran), plus that package's default
	               p-value method - linear interpolation, on sqrt(n)*dip, of
	               its tabulated critical-value grid, the same table R's own
	               dip.test() interpolates. Both were validated rather than
	               trusted: this port's dip statistic and p-value match the
	               diptest PyPI package to machine precision (~1e-16) across
	               336 test vectors - 36 targeted (n=1..36000, unimodal,
	               bimodal, trimodal, constant, heavily-tied) plus a 300-case
	               randomised fuzz sweep - both run again against the exact
	               installed file after two identifier collisions with
	               m_rp_global_var (ns, na, fn - all common enough names that
	               EddyFlow's global-variable module already owns them) forced
	               local renames, to confirm the renames introduced nothing.
	               np.interp's tie-breaking on the critical-value table's
	               duplicate nodes (common at the table's small-n end, where
	               several alpha quantiles share one critical value) had to be
	               reverse-engineered from its actual output rather than read
	               off its docs, which do not specify it: it resolves to the
	               LAST matching node, not the first.
	- Restored: static_checks/test_pwb_reference_static.py's PwbMatchesRflux
	               suite, dropped in 83eadd7 when dyco was not checked out
	               beside this repository. dyco-main is checked out inside it
	               now (as a reference for the separate RFlux gap-analysis
	               work), at exactly the path the removed test expected, and
	               m_pwb_core.f90 has not changed since the drop - so the file
	               was recovered verbatim from git history rather than
	               rewritten. All eleven tests pass unmodified: the unit-root
	               branch decision, the AIC order search (exact integers, up to
	               order 67), the AR coefficients to ten significant digits, the
	               pre-whitened CCF peak and the raw cross-covariance, on both
	               of dyco's fixtures, still agree with RFlux v3.2.0's frozen
	               output. This is the only check in static_checks that would
	               notice the port computing the wrong thing correctly -
	               everything else there reads source text.
	               m_pwb_core.f90's header comment, which had been edited to
	               say the pin was gone, is reverted to match: comment-only,
	               no behavioural change.
	- Fixed: prepass_parallel.f90's header comment claimed the PWB cache
	               pre-pass "is deliberately NOT split", which stopped being
	               true when the streaming classifier's cross-period state
	               (the terminal fallback's lag, the aggregate dataset's
	               membership, the donor tally) moved into
	               PostProcessPwbTimelagCache, run once by the parent over the
	               merged table - the call site in eddyflow-rp_main.f90 already
	               said so and already passes .true. to PlanPrepassBatches for
	               this pass. Comment-only, no behavioural change.
	- New feature: test_pfd, off by default, FCC-only. Ports RFlux's
	               despiking(variant="v1") (Vitale et al. 2020, Biogeosciences)
	               to run once, after a run's NEE/H and LE series are complete:
	               log10(x+1000), an STL decomposition (Cleveland et al. 1990)
	               with RFlux's own parameters (s.window=7, s.degree=0,
	               t.window=mfreq*7+1, t.degree=1, outer=20, inner=2, plus a
	               post-trend "fc" extension), reconstruction back through the
	               inverse transform, then a decile-binned Laplace-distribution
	               outlier test (alpha=0.01) on the residual.
	               m_flux_despike_core.f90 is a from-scratch Fortran port of R's
	               stlplus package (Ryan Hafen, CRAN) - its loess primitive,
	               low-pass filter and jump-interpolation - plus RFlux's own
	               despiking.R on top, ported rather than re-derived for the
	               same reason dip_test.f90 gives: an independent re-derivation
	               of a multi-stage iterative algorithm is exactly where a
	               subtle bug hides from a compiler and from casual review.
	               Validated against R directly rather than assumed - stlplus
	               and despiking.R run locally - at every stage: the loess
	               primitive, the jump-interpolation wrapper, the NA-aware
	               nearest-neighbour window, one isolated cycle-subseries
	               smooth, the full inner/outer robustness loop (with and
	               without missing values, several series lengths and
	               periods), the fc extension, and finally despiking() end to
	               end, all matched to machine precision or an exact spike-flag
	               match across dozens of generated cases.
	               Follows m_pwb_core.f90's discipline: the module is a pure
	               function of its arguments, so it could be reference-tested
	               without linking the rest of the engine.
	               FCC has no whole-run in-memory cache today - Flux1/Flux2/
	               Flux3 are scalars, overwritten every period - so pfd_handle.f90
	               adds one, PfdCache, grown with move_alloc the same way
	               PwbTimelagCache is on the RP side; StorePfdCache appends one
	               row per period from ex_loop, and PostProcessFluxDespiking
	               runs once after the loop closes. Periods per day comes from
	               the modal spacing between sorted periods, not the first
	               pair, so one irregular gap cannot mis-set it; a run under 20
	               periods or under 4 periods/day is skipped rather than fed to
	               a decomposition stlplus itself would refuse (n.p < 4) or
	               despiking() itself calls too short (its own stated minimum
	               is ten days). Output is a new file,
	               ..._flux_despiking....csv, alongside full_output and
	               FLUXNET, with NEE/H/LE plus a spike flag and a cleaned value
	               for each - it does not touch any existing output.
	               Qn (qn_estimator.f90) moved from src_rp to src_common, and
	               PeriodMinutes was extracted out of pwb_timelag_handle.f90
	               into its own file for the same reason: FCC needed both and
	               previously could link neither.
	               The ini key lives in the shared [Project] table
	               (EPPrjCTags(19)), not FCC.SNTags/SCTags: both of those are
	               completely full below their record origins, which cannot
	               move.
	               Regression-swept with the flag at its default (off): 28
	               fixtures, byte-identical.
	- New feature: qc_meth's fourth option, 'vitale_20' (Vitale et al. 2020,
	               Biogeosciences), a two-tier severity flag - 0 (ok), 1
	               (moderate, RFlux's "ModEr") or 2 (severe, "SevEr") - built
	               entirely from diagnostics EddyFlow already computes,
	               regardless of which qc_meth is selected: the ITC deviation
	               (reusing DTFlg's own grade, since Vitale's 30%/100% cutoffs
	               land exactly on two of PartialFlagLF's boundaries) and
	               Mahrt's (1998) nonstationarity ratio, computed
	               unconditionally by RU_Mahrt_98 alongside random
	               uncertainty regardless of which method is chosen there -
	               EddyFlow already had this without knowing it, since
	               RU_Mahrt_98's own sigma_btw/rand_uncer ratio is exactly
	               what RFlux calls M98. Two more feed in only when the
	               corresponding EddyFlow feature is on: KID (kurtosis of
	               increments, computed unconditionally once enabled - see
	               kid.f90, 110bf29) and, when test_rf is also on, AL1/DDI/
	               HF5/HF10/HD5/HD10/DIP.
	               Deliberately narrower than RFlux's cleanFlux(): its
	               low-signal-resolution test and its physical-range filter
	               (hard-coded CO2/H2O/sensible-heat/momentum limits) have no
	               EddyFlow port, and its wind-sector exclusion is left out
	               until EddyFlow supports a time-varying sector - genuine
	               gaps against RFlux, not oversights here.
	               qc_flags_subs.f90's other three methods take StFlg/DtDiff
	               only, computed the same way in RP and FCC; this one also
	               needs Essentials%KID/mahrt98_NR/AL1/DDI/HF5/HF10/HD5/
	               HD10/DIP, which FCC never re-derives from the ex record it
	               reads back (it re-emits test_rf's own columns verbatim,
	               same as the post-flux despiking entry above notes for a
	               different field). QualityFlags takes two new arguments,
	               raw_ok and rf_ok, so RP (which just computed all of this)
	               and FCC (which did not) can each say honestly whether
	               Essentials reflects the period being flagged - without
	               that gate, a period run through FCC would read
	               Essentials's default-initialised zeros as genuine
	               measurements, misfiring AL1 <= 0.5 as severe on every
	               period. Under fcc_follows this grade degrades to the ITC
	               deviation alone for the same reason - and fcc_follows is
	               set for any project using a spectral-correction method
	               beyond the two built-in analytic ones (moncrieff_97,
	               massman_00), which is most real projects, not a rare
	               corner case as first written here. Properly closing this
	               needs FCC's ex record to carry those diagnostics the way
	               it already carries the ITC deviation and the
	               stationarity percentages - a real follow-up, not
	               attempted here; stated plainly in VitaleFlag's own
	               header and the GUI tooltip rather than implied away.
	               Regression-swept with the option at its default
	               (mauder_foken_04): 28 fixtures, byte-identical.
	- New feature: Time-varying invalid wind-sector exclusion (adapted from
	               RFlux's SA_INVALID_WIND_SECTOR_c*/w* site-metadata
	               fields), for a project that already has both wdf_apply
	               and a dynamic metadata file on. Rather than a new file
	               format, this extends the one dynamic metadata already
	               reads: six new optional header columns
	               (wdf_sec1_center/width through wdf_sec3_center/width),
	               each independently optional the same way every other
	               dynamic metadata field is. A record that names the first
	               one replaces the static project sectors outright, up to
	               three, converted into the start/end degrees
	               FilterDatasetForWindDirection already expects; a record
	               that says nothing about wind sectors leaves whatever
	               regime is already in effect - static, or an earlier
	               dynamic record - standing, the rule every other dynamic
	               field already follows. Does not wrap at 0/360, matching
	               FilterDatasetForWindDirection's own start<wd<end test,
	               which does not either - a sector crossing due north
	               needs two entries, the same limitation the static
	               setting already has.
	               Only takes effect when RPsetup%apply_wdf is already on:
	               a dynamic metadata file cannot turn wind-sector exclusion
	               on for a project that left it off, only change which
	               sectors an already-enabled filter uses - so a project
	               with no dynamic metadata file, or one that never uses
	               these six column names, sees no behavioural change at
	               all, byte-identical by construction rather than by
	               having to prove it. No GUI change needed: the dynamic
	               metadata file is already a plain file-path setting with
	               no per-column configuration on that side, so extending
	               its accepted header names is entirely an engine-side
	               change.
	               A live spot check surfaced a scare, not a bug: running
	               it concurrently with an unrelated background regression
	               sweep on this machine produced a SIGSEGV under -j
	               (default parallel workers). Rebuilt with `make debug`
	               (-fbounds-check -fcheck=all) and re-run in isolation -
	               serial and parallel alike, repeatedly - it did not
	               recur; the crash was resource contention from running
	               two heavy multi-process jobs at once, not a fault in
	               this code. Regression-swept in isolation: 28 fixtures,
	               byte-identical.
	- New feature: Per-channel linear calibration (adapted from RFlux's
	               convert_rawdata(): offset + raw*gain, without its
	               quadratic term - see below) on biomet variables read
	               through a metadata file: _gain and _offset, applied in
	               BiometApplyCalibration ahead of the existing unit
	               conversion. Not new tags - biometInitEmbedded already
	               reserved a numeric slot apiece for '_gain' and
	               '_offset' next to every variable's label/id/instrument/
	               unit_in, and BiometVarsType/BiometColType already carry
	               gain/offset fields - but nothing ever read the tags
	               into those fields or applied them; both were live,
	               unused code, most likely staged by this feature's
	               original author for exactly this and never finished.
	               A channel that states neither is left untouched, so a
	               project with no metadata file - or one that never
	               names these two tags - sees no change.
	               Reaches biomet imported through ReadBiometMetaFile: biomet
	               embedded in a .ghg archive, and, once biom_use_native_header
	               routed external biomet through the same metadata-file path
	               (below), external biomet too.
	               No quadratic term, unlike RFlux's own: biometInitEmbedded
	               reserved exactly one numeric slot apiece for gain and
	               offset, nothing more, so a quadratic coefficient has
	               nowhere to live without widening that reservation.
	               Validated three ways: the arithmetic itself (gain-only,
	               offset-only, both, neither, and error-value preservation)
	               against hand-computed expectations in an isolated driver;
	               the full regression suite at the feature's default
	               (unset): 28 fixtures, byte-identical; and, once 7-Zip was
	               on PATH for testing (see below), a live embedded-.ghg run
	               with a hand-built archive (TA raw=50/55/60, gain=2,
	               offset=100 -> 200/210/220 K, averaging to 210 K in the
	               biomet output) - which is what surfaced the scanCsvFile
	               crash fixed below, since no existing fixture had
	               use_biom=1 against embedded .ghg biomet (base_ghg itself
	               has use_biom=0).
	- Bug fix:     A pre-existing, never-previously-exercised SIGSEGV in
	               embedded-.ghg biomet: ReadBiometFile called scanCsvFile -
	               an external procedure with no explicit interface visible
	               at that call site - without its trailing optional
	               `missing` argument. Omitting an optional dummy argument
	               is only well-defined when the caller has an explicit
	               interface for the callee; without one, gfortran passes
	               whatever was already in that argument slot, and
	               present(missing) on the other end can come back true
	               against a bogus address, so the write through it
	               segfaults. init_external_biomet.f90's call to the same
	               routine already passed the argument correctly; only the
	               embedded-.ghg call site omitted it. Confirmed
	               pre-existing rather than introduced this session by
	               reproducing it against the unmodified c419a04 build, with
	               none of this session's changes present. Fixed by passing
	               `missing` explicitly, matching the external-biomet call
	               site. Regression-swept: 28 fixtures, byte-identical (the
	               fixed code path is not on any existing fixture, so this
	               could only have changed output for embedded-.ghg-biomet
	               projects, none of which existed in the suite before now).

8.0.0 (2026-08-20)

    Engine:
	- New feature: cec_min_flux_sigma refuses to partition a flux that is not
	               distinguishable from zero. Zahn et al. (2022) apply no such test and on a
	               well-coupled daytime record none is needed; it is the weak-turbulence case
	               this guards. The octants mean something only if the sign of c' carries a
	               surface signature, and where the scalar is barely coupled to the vertical
	               wind that sign is noise: the moist ejections split near evenly between O1
	               and O2, and the method returns an E/T and a Reco/P split of a signal that
	               is not there. Nothing else in CEC notices - the occupancy gates see two
	               well-filled octants and the components still sum to the total. On the
	               record this came from, night |r(w,CO2)| ran at 0.079 against 0.336 by day,
	               below one sigma. Set it to a number of sigma and a pairing whose water or
	               carbon flux is smaller than that many times its own Finkelstein & Sims
	               random error is refused, reported as status 6 in qc_cec_<species> so that
	               "no resolvable flux" is distinguishable from every other reason a period
	               is dropped. Each further species is judged on its own account, so a
	               carbonyl sulfide flux lost in its own noise rejects itself rather than the
	               pairing that carried it. |F|/RE is about |r|*sqrt(N_indep/2), so this is
	               the significance of that correlation with the number of independent
	               samples taken from the period's own integral timescale rather than
	               assumed. It REJECTS periods rather than rescuing them - the opposite trade
	               to cec_stationarity_mode, and independent of it, applying in either mode.
	               0 is off and is the default, so an untouched project reproduces the
	               published method. It needs ru_meth on; with the estimator off there is no
	               random error to read, and the run says so as Warning(115) and refuses
	               nothing rather than refusing everything.
	- Fix: A column named AGC or RSSI reaches the engine at all. IsCustomOutputColumn
	               listed `agc` and `rssi` beside `ignore` and `flag_1`, so such a column
	               was dropped before it could become a user column - and the two loops
	               that go looking for one, CecSignalColumnFor and SetLicorDiagnostics,
	               search exactly that list. Both were dead code: the conditional eddy
	               covariance signal-strength screen ran on nothing whatever the project
	               asked for, and INST_LI7700_RSSI was always the error value. Neither
	               said so. The exclusion is gone from all three copies of the predicate,
	               each of which now says why it must not come back.
	- New feature: agc_num and agc_<i>_var / _instr / _col in the project file name the
	               signal-strength columns and the analyser each belongs to, on the same
	               shape as the cell and diagnostic records. The screen resolves the
	               column through them first, and reads the record's var lower-cased, so
	               a metadata file spelling the variable `agc` is read the way it was
	               meant - the name comparison this replaces was case-sensitive and
	               failed silently. A project with no agc_num predates the records and
	               still falls back to matching the variable name, so nothing in the
	               field changes behaviour by being left alone. The tags are appended
	               after the cec block, so no existing record origin moves.
	- Fix: A measurement record whose column has since been re-declared is inert rather
	               than honoured. A diagnostic column re-declared as something else left
	               its record behind, and the record was still believed: where it
	               collided with a real diagnostic on the same analyser the run was
	               refused outright - "Two measurement records describe the same quantity
	               on the same instrument" - over a record the interface does not show
	               and the user cannot remove, and where it did not collide it won the
	               slot instead and the engine decoded whatever the column now held as a
	               diagnostic bitfield. RecordNamesColumn compares what the record claims
	               against what the metadata declares, case-insensitively, and the three
	               places that consume a record all ask it. The `ignore` / `not_numeric`
	               exemption that was already there is the same rule, generalised.
	- Fix: The anemometer diagnostic survives a project that carries diagnostic records.
	               ApplyCellDiagRecords accepted only `anemometer_diagnostic` while the
	               interface writes the slug `diag_anem`, so on any project with
	               diag_num > 0 it cleared the anemometer slot and then cycled past the
	               record that would have refilled it. The flags were read and the
	               presence flag was set, and the data was gone. Both spellings are
	               accepted, as they already were in ApplyDiagnosticRecordColumns and
	               DefineUsedVariables.
	- Fix: Each analyser gets its own signal-strength column in the essentials. Every
	               branch of the loop in SetLicorDiagnostics ended it, not only the one
	               that matched its own analyser, so the first AGC or RSSI column of any
	               kind stopped the search: a site with an LI-7500 and an LI-7700 filled
	               one of AGC75 / RSSI77 and left the other at its error value.
	- Fix: An essentials file from an older version is refused with the message that
	               says so, rather than with "no valid data records found". The check on
	               the header ran after InitExVars, which parses the whole file by field
	               position - so every record had already failed and the run had already
	               died by the time the check could speak. It now runs before anything
	               reads a record, on a unit of its own, since at that point none is open.
	               Found by pointing this version at a genuine older file and getting
	               error 61 where error 107 was intended; both directions are now tested.
	- New feature: cec_stationarity_mode chooses which stationarity criterion gates the
	               partition. 0, the default, is Zahn et al.'s and leaves every existing
	               project computing exactly what it computed before. 1 asks instead
	               whether the SPLIT between the two octants holds steady through the
	               period, which is the only thing about a period the partition depends
	               on.
	               Foken's statistic divides by the covariance whose stationarity it is
	               testing, so it runs away as that covariance approaches zero - which
	               for carbon at night it does. On the day this was built against it read
	               755%, 471%, 314% on periods whose octants were perfectly well sampled,
	               and 30 of 48 periods were rejected, 28 of them on that gate, while 26
	               of the 30 had occupancies of 0.24 to 0.44 against a 0.20 requirement.
	               Half a day of sound partitions was being discarded, and it was the
	               half the paper reports the method handling best: at night CEC gives
	               the whole flux to E and R through the sparse-octant rule, which never
	               got to run.
	               The new criterion divides the period into six as StationarityTest
	               does, re-centres each sixth on its own mean as StationarityTest does,
	               and compares the octant split of the averaged sixths against the split
	               of the whole. Both splits are normalised by |f_O1| + |f_O2|, the size
	               of the partition itself, which cannot vanish while the octants hold
	               anything - so the statistic is bounded by 200 and reads as the
	               percentage of its own range by which the split moved. A trend that
	               scales both octants together moves neither split and cancels, which is
	               the robustness a ratio method should have.
	               cec_max_stationarity is still the threshold in either mode and 0 still
	               disables the gate. Fewer than three sixths with enough samples to
	               centre on leaves the statistic undefined, and undefined PASSES:
	               refusing a period on a number that could not be computed is the fault
	               the mode exists to correct, and sparse periods are the occupancy
	               gate's business. Completeness and occupancy gate unconditionally in
	               both modes; this replaces one criterion, not three.
	- New feature: cec_ns_<species> reports that statistic per target, in per cent, in
	               both modes - so the two criteria can be compared on one run and a
	               threshold chosen from data rather than guessed at. Reported for a
	               rejected period too, for the same reason the octant fractions are.
	               BREAKING for the essentials file: the per-target block grows from six
	               fields to seven. A file written before this is refused with Fatal
	               error(107) on the missing CEC_NS_ header marker rather than read one
	               field short per target. The marker is checked only when the header
	               carries a CEC block at all, since a project with the partition off
	               writes none and such a file is not old.
	- Fix: A period the partition rejects reports its octant counts AND its octant
	               fractions, which used to disagree. The counts are accumulated in the
	               sample loop and the fractions were computed after the completeness and
	               stationarity gates, both of which return early - so a rejected period
	               published cec_n_o1 and cec_n_o2 with cec_frac_o1 and cec_frac_o2 at the
	               error value, and no way to divide one by the other, because n_valid is
	               not written out. On the run this was found in, 30 of 48 periods came out
	               that way. They are computed before the gates now. No flux moves: every
	               arm still leaves the targets invalid and ApplyCecDescriptor reads that,
	               not these. The occupancy gate still sits after them, being the one that
	               reads them.
	- Fix: A gas whose every time-lag detection was rejected tries its tube-mate
	               before falling back to a number the rule has already thrown out. The
	               instrument-shared step only borrowed from a donor the rule trusted
	               outright in that same period, which is the right first answer; but a
	               gas like carbonyl sulfide, whose pre-whitened HDI routinely spans the
	               whole search window, then reached the median step with nothing to take a
	               median of and had only its own rejected covariance maximisation left.
	               On the run this was found in, that put COS at 10.6 s in a tube whose
	               delay is 16.2 s, while the CO2 beside it in the same tube carried an
	               interpolated 16.5 s - five periods took a lag 2.8 to 6.2 s wrong. There
	               is now a second shared pass, after the median and before the terminal
	               arm, taking a donor in any settled class rather than only S1 or S2. It
	               reports as S4_instrument_filled, distinct from the S4_instrument_shared
	               of the first pass, because a donor that was itself filled is weaker
	               evidence; and it will not take a lag this same pass just borrowed, so
	               nothing walks from tube-mate to tube-mate. A gas that detects its own
	               lag is untouched.
	- Fix: Builds clean again. import_ascii_with_text declared a datum buffer it
	               never used.
	- Fix: An EddyPro project that states no tlag_meth is imported with
	               tlag_meth=2 rather than with nothing. The key has no
	               *TagFound guard at its reader, so an absent one fell through
	               read_ini_rp's case default to Meth%tlag = 'none' and the run
	               did no time-lag compensation at all, silently. Two is
	               covariance maximization with default - EddyPro's own default
	               and the interface's - so the converted project now runs what
	               the interface displays for it.
	               This is the one entry in the import's defaults table that is
	               NOT a no-op; every other states the value the engine already
	               applies when the key is absent. The table's comment says which
	               and why.
	               A project that DOES state tlag_meth is untouched, as before:
	               the key is in none of the importer's three lists, so the
	               source's choice is copied through verbatim. EddyPro has no
	               fifth method, so an import can never select the pre-whitening
	               block-bootstrap.
	               Deliberately not widened to the reader: a hand-written
	               .eddyflow that omits tlag_meth still gets 'none'.
	- Fix: Conditional Eddy Covariance is off in an imported project, and is
	               now pinned to stay that way. It already was - cec_meth is
	               written 0 whenever the source is silent, and no EddyPro file
	               can state it - but nothing said so, and nothing said the
	               pairing list must stay unwritten either. An absent cec_num
	               means "derive one pairing per CO2 channel from the analyser
	               layout", which is the interface's decision to make once
	               someone opens the converted project, not the importer's.
	               The two importers are now checked against each other: the
	               engine writes these values into the file, while the interface
	               applies its own defaults on load and saves them, and the
	               numbers have to match or the same project computes different
	               fluxes depending on whether it was opened or run.
	- BREAKING: Conditional Eddy Covariance is rebuilt. The cec_* key set, the CEC output
	               column names and the CEC block of the essentials record all change, and
	               ini_version goes to 5.1.0 so an older engine refuses a newer file rather
	               than reading past the parts it does not know.
	- New feature: A project may state which CO2 channel is partitioned against which H2O
	               channel, as cec_num plus cec_<i>_meth / _co2 / _h2o / _extra, and may run
	               several pairings at once. Absent, the layout decides: one pairing per CO2
	               channel, each with the water on its own analyser. That was previously the
	               first record of each species wherever each happened to sit, so a site with
	               two analysers could have its carbon partitioned against the other
	               instrument's water without anything saying so. A pairing that still crosses
	               two analysers is warned about.
	               Output columns carry the analyser their CO2 sits on - E_cec_li7200_1,
	               Reco_cec_miro_mga4_6_2 - with an occurrence number after it when two
	               pairings share one.
	- New feature: Any declared species can be partitioned in a pairing's octants, named in
	               cec_<i>_extra. Zahn et al. define the indicator functions on the vertical
	               wind, the water and the carbon dioxide alone and reuse them unchanged for
	               the second scalar, so nothing about the octants is specific to CO2 - which
	               is what makes carbonyl sulfide a drop-in. Its plant uptake is stomatal and
	               effectively irreversible, so its stomatal component is a cleaner
	               photosynthesis proxy than P_cec. Emitted as <species>_nonstomatal_cec,
	               <species>_stomatal_cec, <species>_total_cec, r_<species>_cec and
	               qc_cec_<species>, in that gas's own flux units.
	- Fix: The hyperbolic threshold H is dimensionless. It was compared against the raw
	               product w's', so what it meant depended on whether the gas was carried in
	               umol/mol or mmol/m3 and no single value was usable anywhere; it is now
	               |w's'| >= H*sigma_w*sigma_s, which is the form Thomas et al. (2008) state
	               and the form in which H = 0.25 means something. The default is still 0,
	               which is what Zahn et al. themselves used, so a project that never set it
	               is unaffected.
	               What the threshold is FOR is leaving out the events nearest the origin,
	               whose sign is noise rather than a surface signature - the failure mode
	               that would otherwise fill both octants about equally and walk the CO2
	               ratio into the r ~ -1 singularity. Whether that helps at a given site is
	               an open question rather than a claim of this release: the paper tested a
	               threshold for CEC and reported the results "not sensitive to the choice
	               of H" at its four sites. It is offered as a usable control, now that a
	               value of it means the same thing everywhere; it is not a fix for weak
	               turbulence. Raising H also thins the octants, so the occupancy limits may
	               need to come down with it.
	- Fix: The singularity band is a setting, cec_singular_band, defaulting to the 0.2
	               Zahn et al. use. It was hard-coded at 0.05 - a quarter of the paper's
	               width, and in contradiction with the repository's own reference model,
	               which had been using 0.2 all along.
	- Fix: An extra species can no longer move the pairing it was added to. A sample
	               counted only where EVERY target had a value, extras included, so adding
	               carbonyl sulfide to a pairing silently revised the E, Tr, Reco and P it
	               had already published - on the test dataset, all four components, both
	               ratios and both octant counts moved. The octants are defined on the
	               vertical wind, the water and the carbon dioxide alone, so those three now
	               decide which samples a period has; a target absent for a sample
	               contributes nothing for that sample and does not remove it from the
	               octant. Each target normalises by its own count, which leaves the ratio -
	               the only thing carried forward - untouched. The completeness gate now
	               applies per target too, so a sparse extra species rejects itself instead
	               of taking the pairing down with it.
	- Fix: A sparse octant is no longer vetoed by the sign check. Where one octant
	               holds fewer than cec_min_octant of the points, Zahn et al. Sec. 2.4 give
	               the whole flux to the other component - which is how the method works at
	               night - but the wrong-sign guard ran ahead of that and could discard it.
	               That branch divides by nothing and leaves the total's sign on the single
	               component it fills, so there was no inversion there for the guard to
	               catch and it only cost periods. It now guards the ratio branch alone.
	- New feature: Warning(114) when the partition is on and the WPL density correction is
	               off. The raw series are converted to mixing ratios only when WPL is
	               requested, and the octants are the SIGNS of the water and carbon
	               fluctuations - in a molar density from an open-path analyser, part of
	               that fluctuation is the air expanding rather than the gas arriving, and
	               it is large enough to reverse the sign. Zahn et al. require the
	               fluctuations themselves to carry the density correction, separately from
	               the totals. A closed-path analyser already reporting mixing ratios is
	               unaffected.
	- Fix: A period whose corrected total points against its own octant sums is flagged
	               rather than split. Substituting the ratio into the partition gives each
	               component the sign of its own sample flux only when the total points the
	               way the two of them point together; otherwise the arithmetic still returns
	               two numbers that sum to the total, and they are a negative respiration
	               beside a positive photosynthesis, or a negative transpiration. Both were
	               being emitted as valid. On the first three hours this was run against, one
	               period produced Reco = -5.13 umol m-2 s-1 flagged normal, and the same
	               period's dewfall was split into a negative evaporation and a negative
	               transpiration. Reported now as qc_cec_* = 5 with the ratio kept.
	- Fix: The signal-strength screen runs BEFORE the gap filler, which is the order
	               Zahn et al. Sec. 3.2 state: samples below the threshold are deleted, then
	               "small gaps (up to four consecutive points) were filled by linear
	               interpolation". The gaps worth repairing are the ones the screen has just
	               made, so a brief dropout now costs four interpolated samples instead of
	               costing the whole period at the 90% completeness gate.
	               This corrects a change that had the two the other way round and
	               described that as the paper's order. It was not: filling first left every
	               condemned sample permanent and counted it against the completeness gate,
	               rejecting periods the reference method keeps. Anyone who prefers that
	               stricter reading - never interpolate across an analyser that was not
	               seeing - gets it exactly by setting cec_max_gap_fill to 0.
	- Fix: The screen runs on the raw series rather than on the finished fluctuations, so
	               the trend is no longer fitted through the samples being rejected. The
	               partition builds its own compact copy of w and its targets to do it, and
	               touches neither E2Set nor E2Primes: the fluxes themselves do not move
	               because a partition asked for a stricter screen.
	- Fix: Each gas is screened against its OWN analyser's diagnostic. The first column
	               named AGC or RSSI on anything matching li7200 or li7500 was used for both
	               gases of the pair, so a two-analyser site screened one analyser's gas with
	               the other's diagnostic, and a quantum cascade laser measuring carbonyl
	               sulfide was not screened at all.
	- Fix: The diagnostic is read in the right direction. RSSI is a received-signal
	               strength, so low is dirty; the AGC of a pre-5.3.0 LI-7500 is the gain the
	               instrument had to apply to see through its windows, so HIGH is dirty. Both
	               are conventionally compared against 70 and the test was "below threshold"
	               whichever it was - so on an old open-path analyser it kept the dirtiest
	               samples and discarded the cleanest.
	- Fix: The partition refuses a period whose columns are not present, rather than
	               reading whatever the freshly allocated E2Primes happened to contain.
	               EliminateCorruptedVariables clears that flag per period, and the detrending
	               routines leave such a column untouched, so the values were arbitrary and
	               often finite enough to be accepted.
	- Fix: The occupancy limits cec_min_o1_o2, cec_min_octant and cec_min_valid are read
	               as percentages and nothing else. The normalizer used to pick between a
	               percentage and a fraction by magnitude, so an interface spin box labelled
	               [%] and set to 0.5 came back as fifty per cent.
	- Fix: The CEC block of the essentials record is read forward off its own count of
	               pairings, like the hygrometer and analyser blocks before it, instead of
	               being found by counting eleven fields back from the end of the row. That
	               anchor made "never append after the descriptor" a rule four files had to
	               keep unaided, and it could not survive a block of variable width. Nothing
	               in that row is anchored to its end any more.
	- Fix: The skipped-period essentials row is the width its own header declares. It
	               wrote three fields per gas where the reader steps over seven, fourteen per
	               analyser where it steps over fifteen, and no hygrometer count at all. The
	               end-anchored CEC block had been absorbing the shortfall into the biomet
	               chunk, which is why nothing had noticed.
	- Fix: The octant counts and fractions reach the full output, not only the essentials
	               file, as cec_n_o1, cec_n_o2, cec_frac_o1 and cec_frac_o2 per pairing. They
	               are what distinguishes a confident partition from a badly sampled one, and
	               the one output most people read could not show it.
	- Fix: CEC component columns carry their gas's own flux scale and label, taken from
	               the caller. A shared helper resolving units through GasUnitIn is right in
	               RP and wrong in FCC, which keeps its own copy - carbonyl sulfide came out
	               of FCC labelled in umol and printed unscaled, a thousandfold error.
	- Fix: cec_meth is parsed from the whole value rather than its first character, and
	               it is now a master switch (0 or 1). Which fluxes a pairing partitions is
	               the pairing's own cec_<i>_meth.
	- Removed: CECFluxType%ok, computed and never read; GPP_cec, computed, never written
	               and carrying photosynthesis's sign; FileType%cec, a path for an RP-to-FCC
	               file that does not exist and never did - the handoff is the essentials row.
	- New feature: The EddyPro import writes complete EddyFlow files, not merely readable
	               ones. It used to produce a project and a metadata the engine accepts but
	               which are not in this format: the interface would migrate both on open and
	               mark them modified before anyone had touched them.
	               The metadata gains the three key families EddyFlow added and nothing else -
	               diffed against the interface's EddyPro 6.2.2 base, those three ARE the whole
	               delta: instr_<K>_ac_freq, instr_<K>_integrates and col_<N>_error_value, for
	               every instrument and column the source describes. The project gains the
	               settings no EddyPro file can state: the eight cec_*, automatic_spectra_config,
	               rot_pf_assessment_only, tlag_assessment_only, the whole
	               [RawProcess_PWBTimelag_Settings] block, and the three keys only the interface
	               reads. The section a key lands in matters even though ReadIniRP sweeps every
	               RawProcess* group as one scope, because the interface reads through QSettings,
	               which groups by section.
	               Every added key carries THE VALUE THIS ENGINE ALREADY APPLIES WHEN THE KEY IS
	               ABSENT - not the interface's default, not a fresh opinion - so stating it is a
	               no-op. Verified rather than asserted: the imported project and its hand-checked
	               native twin produce output byte-identical to each other AND to what they
	               produced before this change. The only file that moves is the copy of the
	               project the run leaves in its output folder, which is the change itself.
	               A key whose ABSENCE is a decision is deliberately still absent, because no
	               value reproduces it: pf_sect_* and wdf_sect_* because presence CREATES a wind
	               sector, gas_<i>_pwb_min_lag/_max_lag because presence sets
	               lag_bounds_provided and set_timelags takes the window from instrument geometry
	               without it, gas_<i>_drift_dir_*/_inv_* because absent means the gas is not
	               drift-corrected and there is no default curve, instr_<K>_max_lack because
	               absent means "follow max_lack", and gas_<i>_fluxnet_default because absent
	               means the lowest record of the species is designated. pf_sect_* and wdf_sect_*
	               are EddyPro keys in any case, present since the fork, and are copied through
	               whenever the source states them
	- Fix: The per-instrument sampling rate and sampling mode are read only when the metadata
	       states them. SearchLocalTags clears CharTags%value before it searches but not
	       NumTags%value, so an unmatched numeric tag keeps whatever the module-level array
	       already held - and instr_<K>_ac_freq and instr_<K>_integrates were the only two
	       numeric reads in that routine taking the value unguarded. "Absent" therefore did not
	       mean "the default", it meant "whatever was last put there". Harmless for a standalone
	       metadata, which is parsed once; for a GHG archive, where one metadata is parsed per
	       raw file through the same ANTags, file N inherited file N-1's rate and sampling mode.
	       Nothing needed defaulting on the new false arm - Instr = NullInstrument already
	       leaves ac_freq at the error code, which ColumnAcFreq reads as "the file's rate" like
	       any value <= 0, and integrates at .false., which is instantaneous. No fixture moves
	- Fix: The imported metadata states ;GHG_METADATA rather than carrying the source's first
	       line through. That is what DlProject::saveProject emits unconditionally; the
	       EDDYFLOW spelling is accepted on read and never written
	- Change: PWB detection runs on rotated, pre-WPL data, and the option to choose is retired.
	          7.2.5 introduced pwb_detect_prewpl and it never actually ran: the interface wrote the
	          same setting as pwb_detect_on_raw, SearchLocalTags matches tag labels by exact
	          equality, and so the checkbox reached nothing for every project ever saved. Renaming
	          it would have switched on, for everybody at once, a pass that had never executed.
	          It is retired instead, because there was never a choice worth offering. Both
	          alternatives ran on rotated 20 Hz data; the only difference was whether the gas series
	          had already been through the pointwise mixing-ratio conversion, and that conversion
	          runs BEFORE time-lag compensation - so detecting after it puts the cell temperature
	          and water signals into the gas series at the wrong relative lag, and the gas series is
	          the one being cross-correlated.
	          Checked rather than assumed: over a three-hour run every one of the thirteen reliable
	          S1/S2 detections is bit-identical either way, and the rows that move are the ones PWB
	          itself rejected as unreliable. Both spellings are removed from any project the EddyPro
	          import writes, so no dead key is left behind
	- Fix: pwb_smoothing_width agreed on 5. The engine applies 5 when the key is absent and the
	       interface defaulted to 6 - and since the interface writes the key unconditionally,
	       every project it saved carried a 6 while a project without the key got a 5, with
	       nothing anywhere to say the two disagreed. The interface defaults to 5 now. A project
	       already saved keeps whatever it states; only a new one changes
	- New feature: A check on the interface side reads BOTH repositories and fails if a PWB key
	               it writes is absent from this engine's tag table, or if a shared default
	               disagrees. Nothing at build time connects the two, so a name or a default can
	               drift with both sides compiling and running - which is exactly how both of the
	               two fixes above went unnoticed
	- New feature: An EddyPro project can be run directly. Hand `eddyflow_rp` a *.eddypro
	               file - or any project whose first line says `;EDDYPRO_PROCESSING` - and it
	               is converted into an ordinary EddyFlow pair beside the file it came from,
	               `<name>_ep_imported.eddyflow` and `<name>_ep_imported.metadata`, and run.
	               Nothing downstream knows an import happened. The source files are never
	               written, and the import runs once: RP edits the project it ran and FCC
	               reads that edit back, so a second import on the FCC run would send FCC to
	               the EddyPro file's stale ex_file. An imported project already on disk is
	               reused, and the run says so.
	               Two things could not be read before. InitEnv discarded a positional path
	               that was not *.eddyflow, and a project stating no gas_num has been refused
	               outright since format 5.0.0 - which is the shape of every EddyPro file
	               there is. Of the keys in one, all but about ninety are read verbatim under
	               the same name; those ninety are the four fixed gas slots with their
	               per-species settings, which become records. The mapping is the interface's,
	               not a new one, and a static check reads the interface's own tables so the
	               two repositories cannot drift apart unnoticed.
	               The fourth slot's species is recoverable only from the metadata - the
	               project states col_n2o and nothing else - so a COS column declared there
	               would otherwise be given nitrous oxide's molecular weight, wrong by a
	               third, with nothing in the output looking unusual. EddyPro also spells that
	               slot two ways within one file (n2o in the parameter settings and in
	               out_full_sp_*/out_full_cosp_w_*, gas4 in the spectral, time-lag and
	               out_raw_* families); both are accepted everywhere.
	               A setting the project does not state is not written, because the engine
	               applies a record override whenever the tag is present and 0 is a
	               meaningful absolute limit, default time lag and minimum flux. Nothing
	               EddyFlow-only is invented either - cec_*, the PWB block, the planar-fit
	               sectors are all *TagFound-guarded with defaults, and writing those defaults
	               would dress them up as decisions somebody made.
	- Fix: A Campbell site's instrument key is rewritten everywhere it appears, not only where
	       the reader already handles it. `csat3` and `csat3b` became `csi_csat3` and
	       `csi_csat3b`, and a model key sits in three places: the metadata's instr_<K>_model,
	       the metadata's col_<N>_instrument, and the project's master_sonic. Only the first is
	       canonicalised on read; the other two are compared by index() against a model that
	       has been, so index('csat3_1', 'csi_csat3_1') is zero. The columns keep a null
	       instrument and metadata validation rejects the file - for a GHG archive, every file
	       in the run - and no column is flagged master_sonic, taking the sonic axis
	       adjustment, the angle-of-attack and w-boost overrides and the anemometric block of
	       DefineE2Set with it. The import rewrites all three. The regression fixture is a
	       CSAT-3B for exactly this reason: a Gill one would pass either way.
	       Scoped to the import: the same mismatch is still reachable by a hand-written
	       native .metadata that spells a Campbell model the old way, which is a reader
	       fix rather than an import one
	- New feature: The import names any instrument model this engine does not know, once per
	               file. That used to surface only as ExceptionHandler(25) per raw file, with
	               nothing to say which key was at fault
	- New feature: Gas/cell/diagnostic column records (EddyFlowProj%gas, %cell, %diag) replace the four fixed gas slots, so the same species can be measured by more than one instrument
	- New feature: Project and metadata tag tables are now generated by prj/gen_project_tags.py and prj/gen_metadata_tags.py instead of hand-numbered
	- New feature: Overflow sentinel detects metadata describing more instruments than the engine can hold, instead of silently dropping them
	- New feature: Cell temperature/pressure blocks are now one per instrument, and every gas is
	               density-corrected in the cell its record names. Verified on CH-LAE against two
	               v7.2.5 runs: the LI-7200 gases reproduce the LI-7200-only run and the MIRO gases
	               the MIRO-only run to within 0.35% on every flux, while N2O - which v7.2.5 gave
	               the LI-7200's cell, 13.5x too dense - moves as expected
	- New feature: Each gas is WPL-corrected with the water its record names, and each hygrometer
	               carries its own moisture regime; a second hygrometer reports its own H, LE, ET,
	               TAU, MO_LENGTH and ZL
	- New feature: A column legend file naming, for every output column, the species, the instrument
	               and the raw column behind it
	- New feature: Each block of the spectral assessment file states the species and analyser it
	               was fitted for (`var=`/`instr=`, written past `fc` so older builds ignore it),
	               and the reader resolves a block by that before falling back to the block name.
	               Block names are ordinals over repeats of a species - CO2_1 is whichever CO2
	               record comes first - so a project that lists its records in another order used
	               to hand one analyser's transfer function to the other
	- Fix: The second hygrometer's RH block is named for the tag alone. It was written as
	       "<TAG> vapour TFP" and the reader takes everything before TFP as the block name, so the
	       block called itself "H2O_2 VAPOUR", matched nothing, and was consumed and discarded on
	       every read - the second hygrometer was fitted and thrown away, which is what writing the
	       block was meant to stop. Files already carrying the old header still resolve
	- Fix: The unnamed primary-water table is stamped too, and read onto the hygrometer it names
	       rather than onto whichever record the reading project calls primary
	- New feature: The RH/cut-off relation is fitted per hygrometer, and each hygrometer's block
	               carries its own coefficients (`exp=`, again past `fc`). The correction
	               evaluates exp(A*RH^2 + B*RH + C), and A/B/C were one project-wide set fitted
	               from the primary - so a second hygrometer's nine fitted cut-offs were written,
	               read back and then overridden by the primary's curve. Each analyser's own path
	               type now decides which arm fits it, and the project-wide set remains the
	               fallback for a hygrometer the assessment never reached
	- Fix: Species molecular weights carry six figures, on the atomic weights the table already
	       used. CO2 and N2O weigh nearly the same and were both written as 44.01, so the one
	       place a gas's identity is stated in numbers could not tell them apart; they are
	       44.0095 and 44.0128. CH4 16.0425, H2O 18.0153 and dry air 0.0289647 moved with them.
	       Water and dry air had to move together: mu = Md/MW_H2O and their roundings cancelled,
	       so refining water alone would have made the WPL ratio less accurate than before.
	       Effect: LE, ET and the ambient humidity terms shift by ~0.026%, every flux by
	       ~0.0005% through mu, and a gas measured as a mass density by its own weight change
	- Fix: A hygrometer reports what it measured. The biomet RH used to be written over the primary
	       hygrometer's chi/r/d, so on a two-hygrometer site which instrument's number was real
	       followed the primary designation. The biomet humidity is now reported in its own right as
	       h2o_biomet_mole_fraction/_mixing_ratio/_molar_density, and the corrections that used it
	       still use it
	- Fix: err_label tag collision and missing out_full_sp_gas4 key in the metadata tag tables
	- Fix: Mark gas/cell/diagnostic record columns as used so they survive column pruning and reach DefineE2Set
	- Fix: The RP evapotranspiration and drift corrections read the primary hygrometer's own cell
	       temperature, pressure and pressure covariance instead of the first instrument's, which is
	       what FCC has always done - the two disagreed for a site whose hygrometer is not on cell
	       record one
	- Fix: CopyFile closes both units, and EditIniFile checks its opens before the close that
	       deletes its input. FCC copies the project into the output folder and then edits that
	       copy, and the copy came out zero bytes on every run where the automatic spectral
	       configuration had a recommendation to write
	- Fix: Unit conversion leaves the error code alone. `error` is -9999, and three arms of
	       ConvertTraceGasUnits scaled the whole column, so a missing sample in a ppb, ppt or
	       pmol_mol gas came out as -9.999 - past CleanUpE2Set's -300 test, past every
	       "/= error" check downstream, and into the flux as a plausible mixing ratio. It
	       reached any sample left at the error code, an unread row or a short file included.
	       The six arms beside them were already guarded. No regression fixture moves: the
	       test dataset carries no error code for these arms to scale
	- Fix: A record with one unparseable field is no longer discarded whole. The CH-LAE files
	       carry a quoted "NAN" in the analyser's gas columns - 1202 of 36001 rows in one file -
	       and every such record was thrown away, wind data included: used_records per half hour
	       read 17474 of 18000 and SONIC_NR 17471. The whole-record read stays, because it
	       carries millions of rows; only a record it cannot parse is walked field by field, and
	       then one field's worth is lost instead of the record. Each gas now loses exactly its
	       own NAN samples. Every flux moves - this is a re-baselining, reconciled column by
	       column in tests/regression/README.md
	- New feature: Whatever a file writes for "no reading" is recognised in the units the file is
	               written in, before anything scales it. NaN and infinity are caught by
	               self-inequality rather than by comparing to a sentinel - a NaN reads as a
	               perfectly good real and passed every "/= error" test downstream - and a column
	               can declare its own fill through col_<N>_error_value, which only ADDS to the
	               values that always mean missing: -9999, NaN, an unparseable token, an empty
	               field. An absent declaration means the built-ins alone
	- New feature: Warning(109) when a gas states no absolute limits, said once per run. The test
	               already declined to run and flagged the gas 9, but a digit in a flag string is
	               not where someone looks when a flux is suspect
	- New feature: A run log. Each binary writes everything it prints to a .log beside the
	               other outputs, named the same way - project id, run timestamp, run-mode tag.
	               A run used to leave a dozen files behind and no record of what the engine
	               said while producing them: which warnings fired, which periods were skipped
	               and why. That record existed only in whatever terminal the run happened in,
	               and under the interface it did not exist at all. The console is untouched -
	               this is a tee, not a redirect, because the interface reads standard output
	               to drive its progress display. Verified line for line against a capture of
	               the console taken before the change; the only two lines the log lacks are
	               gfortran's own "STOP" and IEEE notice, which the engine never emits
	- Fix: SpectralAnalysis no longer writes through its intent(in) argument. It handed the
	       caller's array straight to Tapering and FourierTransform, both intent(inout), and
	       compiled only because those two have implicit interfaces - the binned pass now
	       transforms a copy. No output moves; nothing read the array afterwards
	- New feature: A column sampled below the file's rate has its spectrum and its cospectrum
	               with w rebuilt from its own samples, on its own grid, instead of from the
	               linear interpolation that fills its missing rows. The interpolation is a
	               low-pass filter and it showed: on the slow-instrument fixture the analyser's
	               CO2 read 0.242 near its cut-off interpolated against 0.623 rebuilt. Decimating
	               by an integer factor over the same span leaves the frequency axis unchanged,
	               so the result occupies the same bins; nothing above a column's own Nyquist is
	               reported at all. Single-rate projects are byte-identical
	- New feature: How the vertical wind is paired with a slower instrument's samples follows
	               instr_<K>_integrates: the w at that instant by default, or w averaged over the
	               interval the sample closes when the instrument integrates - which is what the
	               instrument itself did to the gas. Instantaneous is the default because pairing
	               an averaged wind against a point-sampled gas biases the covariance. The
	               Metadata File Editor offers it per instrument, beside the acquisition frequency
	- Fix: The FCC cut-off validation uses each hygrometer's own Nyquist instead of the station's.
	       A hygrometer slower than the station resolves nothing above its own half-rate, so the
	       station's bound accepted fitted cut-offs it could not have measured, by exactly the
	       ratio of the two rates. FCC has no metadata file, so the FLUXNET analyser block now
	       carries the rate RP resolved for each gas (<TAG>_INSTR_AC_FREQ). Purely additive:
	       one column per analyser, every pre-existing cell byte-identical, and an ex record
	       written without the column falls back to the station's rate
	- Fix: The spectra feasibility test allows a third of what the COLUMN should have produced
	       rather than a third of the file's rows, and the longest-gap duration discounts the
	       rows between one sample of a slower instrument and the next. Measured against the
	       rows, a 1 Hz column in a 10 Hz file was excluded from the spectra whatever its data
	       said, and reported a permanent 0.9 s gap that never happened
	- New feature: An instrument can state the rate it samples at (instr_<K>_ac_freq in the
	               .metadata) and how much of its own expected data may be missing
	               (instr_<K>_max_lack in [RawProcess_Settings]). Completeness is measured
	               against what that instrument should have produced rather than against the
	               file's rows, so a 1 Hz analyser in a 10 Hz file is complete rather than 90%
	               missing. Before this, such a column was dropped outright and its flux was
	               -9999. Both settings are optional and absent means "the file's rate" and
	               "the project-wide max_lack", so no existing project moves - every regression
	               fixture is byte-identical. The project-wide setting now means the anemometer's
	               allowance and the file's own completeness
	- Fix: Spike detection counts consecutive samples, not consecutive rows. A run was
	       extended across the error rows a slower column carries between its samples, and the
	       total compared against sr_num_spk, so a single-sample spike in a 1 Hz analyser in a
	       10 Hz file counted as ten: with the default allowance of three, every run in a column
	       at a quarter of the file's rate or slower was discarded as a physical excursion and
	       the column was never despiked, reporting no spikes rather than "not assessed". An
	       accepted run's interpolation also wrote its whole row span, turning the error rows
	       inside it into fabricated samples - which let a period with under 90% real data pass
	       the CEC validity gate. The interpolation now draws its line in row distance from the
	       last good sample and writes sample rows only, and the sr_hf_lim percentage is taken
	       over the column's own samples instead of the file's rows. A column with no missing
	       values is byte-identical; on CH-LAE, where the gas columns carry NAN, a spike beside
	       a gap was detected and then left in the record - CO2 kurtosis 1638 against 8.07 once
	       it is removed
	- Fix: The exponential running mean computes the trend for its own column. The
	       assignment inside the loop over columns subscripted every column, guarded on one of
	       them, so each pass overwrote the others using that column's choice of lag and folded
	       in whatever the row held elsewhere. At a single rate the assignment was identical on
	       every pass and the mistake was idempotent; once columns sample at different rates, a
	       fast column's pass writes the error code from a slower column's missing rows into
	       that column's trend, and only the last column present survives it. Affects the 'ew'
	       detrending option alone
	- New feature: The spectral assessment succeeds per gas. It was all or nothing: the file was
	               written only if the primary hygrometer had a usable RH class, so a site whose water
	               could not be fitted lost the blocks of the gases that had been - and at correction time
	               one unfitted gas, or an unfitted RH regression, demoted every gas to Moncrieff. On a
	               one-day CH-LAE assessment that cost CO2, N2O and COS their in-situ corrections because
	               nine RH classes cannot be filled from 48 half-hours. The file is now written whenever
	               any gas was fitted, each gas is corrected by the method its own data supports, and the
	               gases that cannot be corrected in situ go through the analytic method instead of
	               dragging the rest down with them
	- Fix: The high-frequency method no longer sticks. The per-period fallback to Moncrieff was
	       written back into the project's own setting, so the first period that could not be
	       corrected in situ demoted every period after it for the rest of the run, whatever their
	       own data looked like. The decision is now local to the period that makes it
	- New feature: Warning(110) when the assessment file covers some gases and not others, raised
	               whether the file was just written or read from disk. A file covering four gases out of
	               six looks exactly like a file covering all six, and the difference decides whether half
	               the fluxes carry an in-situ correction
	- New feature: The readiness report says what each gas actually gets - in situ or analytically
	               - and reports PARTIAL as its own outcome rather than calling it SUCCESS or FAILED
	- Fix: The report stops recommending a flux limit that cannot help. A gas rejected for small
	       fluxes is fixed by lowering the floor; a gas whose classes are too thinly populated is
	       not, and the suggested floor - a percentile of what is already there - removes records
	       when it lands above the current one, which is what it did for water at CH-LAE. The two
	       failures now read differently: the second names the best class occupancy reached and
	       points at the assessment window or sa_min_smpl
	- New feature: A path the project states, which does not exist, now stops the run. Five settings
	               used to answer a missing path by quietly computing something else: a missing planar
	               fit file became double rotation, a missing time-lag file became covariance
	               maximisation, and a missing spectral assessment file, binned co-spectra directory or
	               full co-spectra directory became Moncrieff 1997. The run finished and the numbers
	               were labelled as the method that had been asked for, which is what made it dangerous
	               - the first two change every flux in the run. The engine already stopped for three
	               other missing inputs (the project file, the alternative metadata file, the raw data
	               directory); these five now do the same. The message names the setting, the path as
	               given, and what to change - either correct the path, or choose the interface option
	               that makes EddyFlow produce the thing itself. A path the project does not state is
	               unaffected
	- Fix: The spectral fit arrays no longer overrun on a project with no water. xFit, yFit
	       and ddum are one global set shared by FitRHtoCutoff and FitTFModels, sized
	       differently by each under the same "only if not already allocated" test.
	       FitRHtoCutoff claimed them at a fixed 10 and then returned early when the project
	       had no primary water, without freeing them, so the next FitTFModels found them
	       allocated, skipped its own sizing and indexed past the end - a crash. It could only
	       bite a project with no water, and those never reached it while a missing
	       binned-spectra directory was quietly demoting every run to Moncrieff
	- Fix: A biomet file that is not there is reported instead of created. scanCsvFile opened its
	       input without status='old', so a path that did not exist was CREATED, empty; the open then
	       succeeded, the scan found no rows, and its own "too few rows or columns" guard fired. The
	       run announced a content problem for a file that was simply absent, carried on without
	       biomet data, and left an empty file behind in the working directory.
	       That is not hypothetical: a project whose biomet path had been corrupted upstream produced
	       exactly this, and the only trace was a stray file named after the wreckage of the path.
	       Two further biomet opens had the same shape and are closed with it; the fourth already had
	       status='old', which is what showed the other three to be oversights rather than policy
	- Fix: A project that NAMES a biomet file and cannot open it stops, through the same
	       AbortOnMissingPath every other user-named path already uses - pf_file, to_file, sa_file
	       and proj_file all abort, and biomet was the one that degraded quietly. The message names
	       the setting, quotes the path as the project gave it, and says what to do about it. Only
	       for an explicitly named file: one that came from a biomet DIRECTORY listing existed a
	       moment ago, so its disappearance is a different and much rarer event and is reported
	       without taking the run down
	- Change: scanCsvFile can say whether a file was absent or merely unusable. Without that the
	          caller cannot tell a wrong path from a bad file, which is why the original message had
	          to hedge between them and ended up describing the wrong one
	- Change: Thirty-eight regression fixtures declared an external biomet file on a drive that does
	          not exist. They were never reading biomet - the run logged Error(2), then Error(71),
	          then continued without it - so they now state use_biom=0, which is what they actually
	          do. The two fixtures that genuinely exercise biomet name a file that is present and are
	          untouched. A new static check keeps the two in step: a fixture either names a biomet
	          file it can open, or says it uses none
	- Fix: An even PWB smoothing width uses the window it names. The centred rolling mean summed
	       2*(width/2)+1 terms and divided by that, so an even width became a symmetric window one
	       sample WIDER than asked for - a different filter, not a rescaling, and free to move the
	       argmax it feeds. R, zoo and dyco all take exactly `width` terms, an odd window being
	       symmetric and an even one putting its extra sample after the centre; dyco verified that
	       against R for widths 4, 5 and 6.
	       This is not a corner: the paper's smoothing width is hz/2 + 1, which is 6 at 10 Hz. An
	       odd width is unaffected, so the default of 5 and every project using it produce
	       identical numbers
	- Fix: S2 no longer accepts a detection for sitting close to a lag the carry limit has already
	       expired. S2 exists to accept an uncertain detection that agrees with a trusted
	       neighbour, and leaning on an anchor judged too old to hand out walks the series along on
	       evidence that no longer counts - which reintroduced the unbounded carry through the
	       classifier while the fills below it expired correctly. With pwb_max_carry_h defaulting
	       to 24 h this was live on every run
	- New feature: The reference test covers the smoother. No frozen R value touches it - the
	               pinned quantities come from the unsmoothed cross-correlation and from the raw
	               cross-covariance - which is exactly how the even-width window fixed above went
	               unnoticed by a check that agreed with RFlux to twelve significant digits. The
	               driver gained a mode that smooths a synthetic series, and the test compares it
	               against zoo's rule written out independently, at widths 4, 5 and 6
	- Note: PWB's arithmetic WAS pinned to the original R implementation during this
	               release's development, and no longer is. dyco shipped the fixtures and the
	               frozen output of RFlux v3.2.0's tlag_detection() for two cases, one on each
	               branch of the unit-root test; a driver ran this engine's own chain over them
	               and compared the AR order chosen by AIC, the AR coefficients, the peak of the
	               pre-whitened cross-correlation and the raw cross-covariance, all agreeing to
	               twelve significant digits. Running it for the first time found three defects
	               that no text-matching check could have seen, and all three fixes are below
	               and are staying.
	               The comparison itself is removed with dyco, which is no longer checked out
	               beside this repository - the fixtures and the expected values both lived
	               there, so the checks could only skip. Nothing replaces it: no check now
	               verifies these numbers, only the source text that produces them. The head
	               of static_checks/test_pwb_reference_static.py records what it covered and
	               how to restore it. The driver stays, and still proves m_pwb_core links
	               without engine state.
	- Fix: The raw cross-covariance is read off the UNDIFFERENCED series, linearly detrended.
	       Differencing feeds the AR filters only; taken off the differenced arrays the number
	       is a covariance of increments, which on a drifting record is smaller by two orders of
	       magnitude and free to carry the opposite sign. dyco had this bug and fixed it. The
	       branch is not the rare one it sounds: it fires whenever sonic temperature drifts over
	       an averaging period, which is most of them
	- Fix: The cross-covariance divides by N, as R's ccf(type="covariance") does, and centres
	       each series once on its own full-length mean. Dividing by each lag's overlap count
	       instead inflated it by N/(N-|lag|) - 2.9% at lag 169 of 6000
	- Fix: Differencing returns N-1 values. It wrote them back over 2..N and set the first to
	       zero, keeping the length at N and feeding the AR fit one fabricated sample; at
	       N = 6000 that moved the first AR coefficient in the sixth significant digit
	- Fix: The full-data pre-whitened CCF drops the AR-initialisation samples, as R's
	       na.action = na.omit does. Kept as zeros they shifted every mean and sum that
	       followed, which at an AR order of 67 moved the correlation in the fourth significant
	       digit. The bootstrap still keeps them, which is also what R does
	- Fix: A time lag is never borrowed across instruments. It is a property of one sampling
	       tube, so sharing one between analysers is not an approximation of the right answer
	       but a different measurement. The identity test now requires that BOTH records name an
	       instrument and that the names match: there is no fall-back to the model string, which
	       cannot tell two LI-7200s at one site apart, and a record naming no instrument neither
	       donates nor receives, because nothing then proves it shares a tube. The run log says
	       so when a gas is in that position.
	       Three places could hand a lag from one gas to another and only two of them asked any
	       question at all: the aggregate summary had no instrument test whatsoever, so on a
	       multi-analyser site it could give one tube's optimised window to a gas measured down
	       another - and that file is read back by the next run
	- Fix: A failed H2O relative-humidity class table no longer switches the whole run to
	       covariance maximization when the method is PWB. Under PWB that aggregate table is a
	       by-product; the method's output is the half-hourly table, already complete when the
	       switch fired. It discarded all of it, processed every period with covariance
	       maximization and reported every _TLAG_PWB_SOURCE column as missing. The classes need
	       reliable water detections, and a hygrometer that has none says nothing about the
	       other gases' lags
	- Fix: The applied time lag reported for a period is the one the data actually moved by,
	       measured back off the record shift. Interpolation makes fractional lags ordinary and
	       the data can only move by whole records, so the requested and the applied value now
	       differ by up to half a sample as a matter of course
	- Change: The bootstrap block length is derived per gas as max(configured, 2 x widest search
	          bound), following R's l = 2*LAG.MAX coupling and dyco's floor on it. A block
	          shorter than the lag range cannot contain the structure the bootstrap exists to
	          preserve; this warned about exactly that and then used the short block anyway. On
	          a project with a 25 s window and the default 20 s block every gas was resampling
	          under-blocked while the log said so and nothing acted on it. Detections improve
	          markedly as a result
	- New feature: pwb_max_carry_h bounds how far a lag may travel to a period that detected
	               none, defaulting to 24 hours; zero restores the published rule, under which
	               one reliable half hour can supply days. It bounds interpolation, the forward
	               carry and the backward fill with one value, because bounding a single
	               direction achieves nothing - with detections either side of a long unusable
	               stretch the later one would fill from the future exactly the span the earlier
	               was forbidden to cross. Past it the period falls to a same-analyser donor,
	               and the row is classed S3_expired.
	               Measured in elapsed hours where dyco counts averaging periods: our table has
	               a row only where a period was processed, so a period count would reach
	               straight across a gap in the raw files
	- Change: The FLUXNET _TLAG_PWB_SOURCE column has a code for every way a period can reach its
	          lag. Interpolated, back-filled and median-filled periods have none in the older
	          scheme, so they fell through to the default arm and reported no source at all
	- Change: The arithmetic moved to m_pwb_core, which depends on nothing but the kinds module,
	          and states its deliberate departures from dyco and RFlux in one place - the guard band
	          against their na.locf edge fill, the preference for a combination that did not land on
	          the window edge, the carry limit counted in elapsed hours rather than averaging
	          periods, the degenerate-width fallback, and the moving-block bootstrap against R's
	          wrapping one. A difference nobody wrote down is indistinguishable from a defect the
	          next time the two are compared.
	          The reference driver links that module and one other object and nothing else, so it
	          stops linking the moment a routine in the chain reaches for PWBSetup or the column
	          table - which is what keeps the numbers reproducible outside a full run, and firmer
	          than any scan of the source. It builds into the object directory, not bin/, because it
	          is a test fixture and an unshipped binary beside the two real ones invites somebody to
	          ship it
	- New feature: PWB settles every averaging period's time lag with the whole run in hand. The
	               classifier ran while periods streamed past, so it could only look backwards: a
	               period before the first reliable detection had nothing to carry forward and fell
	               back to covariance maximisation, and a gap in the middle held the last good lag
	               flat for as long as the gap lasted. Neither was a limit of the method, only of
	               not having read the rest of the run yet.
	               The pre-pass has read it. Each period is now settled from the stored evidence in
	               the reference implementation's order: the HDI pre-filter, then S1, then S2, then
	               the gas's OWN lag in all three of its forms - interpolated between the reliable
	               lags either side, carried forward, filled backward - and only then a lag borrowed
	               from another gas on the same analyser, then that gas's median, then the fallback
	               the pass recorded.
	               The gas's own lag comes first deliberately: two gases down one tube still have
	               measurably different delays, a systematic 0.35 s between CH4 and N2O being
	               ordinary, so borrowing trades a stale number for a biased one. A fill_method
	               column names which arm settled each row, so the file says how every half-hour was
	               decided and not only what it decided. Live detection, with no pre-pass, keeps the
	               causal classifier; nothing better is possible when the future has not been read
	- Fix: pwb_hdi_prefilter_s does something. It has been in the interface, in the project file
	       and in the settings fingerprint since PWB was added, and no computation has ever read
	       it - so a detection with a very wide HDI could still be accepted by S2 merely for
	       landing near the previous period's lag. It is applied before classification now, which
	       is what the reference does and what its name has always promised
	- Fix: The bootstrap draws an independent resampling per period. The seed was built from the
	       gas and the combination only, so every half-hour drew the identical sequence of block
	       start positions - one fixed template applied to the whole run rather than a draw. The
	       generator itself was glibc's LCG constants (chosen for modulus 2^31) used with modulus
	       2^31-1 and then reduced off the low bits, which are the worst-correlated bits an LCG
	       has; a replicate draws about ninety starts in a row from them, so replicates resembled
	       one another, the HDI came out too narrow, and S1 was handed to detections that had not
	       earned it. It is xorshift64 read from the top bits now, seeded from the period as well,
	       so a period stays reproducible while periods are independent of each other
	- Change: The two PWB "speed options" are retired. Neither was one. Skipping the CCF
	          normalisation saved two passes over the series against one per lag, well under a
	          percent, and the divisor is constant across lags - so within a combination it
	          changed nothing, while ACROSS combinations it changed everything: the four are
	          compared at their modes, and they pair the scalar against vertical wind and against
	          sonic temperature, whose covariances carry different physical units. Unnormalised,
	          the winner was decided by that unit scale rather than by peak prominence, on every
	          period. Capping the AR order saved about as little and under-fits the pre-whitener,
	          which is the step that sharpens the peak the method exists to find
	- Change: The cross-correlation is evaluated over the declared window plus a guard band, not
	          over the mirrored symmetric range the window sits inside. Half the smoothing width
	          is what the centred rolling mean consumes; the two seconds on top exist because the
	          window is a guess, and a peak just outside it used to be indistinguishable from an
	          ordinary edge-pinned failure. The applied lag still comes from inside the declared
	          window - the guard band informs, it does not widen the search - and two new columns
	          plus a run-log warning report when the peak keeps landing outside, which says the
	          window is wrong rather than the detector.
	          With that and the bootstrap scratch buffers hoisted out of the resampling loop, a
	          three-hour eight-gas run with [-5, 25] s windows takes 70 s against 97 s, measured
	          best-of-two on the same input
	- Change: PWB writes two files instead of four. The per-period diagnostics repeated the
	          half-hourly table one column apart and the per-gas summary held tallies the run log
	          already prints; both are folded away. What remains is the half-hourly table - which is
	          also the file a later run reads back, so a run can be replayed exactly from it - and
	          the aggregate _pwb_timelag_opt.
	          The table names its gases rather than numbering their slots, and carries the settled
	          lag beside the evidence for it: carry_hours, the unit-root decision with the three AR
	          orders it produced, and the pre-whitened diagnostics tlag_pw_s, corr_pw and cv_99 -
	          R's Bartlett band, below which the pre-whitened correlation is not distinguishable
	          from noise however narrow the bootstrap interval looks. It is version 4; every earlier
	          version predates a setting that is now in the fingerprint and could not have matched
	          this build in any case

